{"id": "cur-essay-168", "group": "gb-essay-168.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the historical foundation of law enforcement and its role in safeguarding citizens from civil disorder and crime. Examine the principles and responsibilities of peace officers in ensuring the safety, possession, and health of the community.", "text": "Organized law enforcement is a younger institution than most people assume. For much of history, keeping the peace was a communal duty rather than a profession. In medieval England, households were grouped into tithings whose members answered for one another's conduct, and a victim raising the \"hue and cry\" could oblige neighbors to join the pursuit of a suspect. The shire reeve, ancestor of the modern sheriff, represented the crown locally, while unpaid constables and night watchmen handled routine order. Colonial America borrowed these arrangements, and in the southern colonies slave patrols added a harsher form of policing whose legacy still shapes debates about the institution.\n\nIndustrialization exposed the weakness of the amateur system. Crowded cities produced theft, riots, and disorder that watchmen could not manage, and governments often answered with soldiers, sometimes with deadly results. Sir Robert Peel's Metropolitan Police Act of 1829 offered an alternative: a uniformed, salaried, civilian force whose purpose was to prevent crime rather than simply punish it. Boston, New York, and other American cities built similar departments within two decades. The principles associated with Peel remain the moral foundation of the profession. Police succeed only with public approval, they should use force as a last resort and in the smallest necessary amount, and their effectiveness is measured by the absence of crime and disorder, not by the visibility of police action.\n\nFrom these roots flow the responsibilities of today's peace officers. They protect life by responding to violence, accidents, and emergencies. They protect possessions by deterring and investigating theft, fraud, and vandalism. They also protect community health in a broader sense, enforcing traffic and safety laws, assisting people in mental health or substance crises, and supporting public health measures during disasters. Carrying out these duties demands impartiality, restraint, honesty, and respect for constitutional rights. When officers honor those obligations, they earn the trust that makes voluntary compliance possible. When they do not, the arrangement Peel envisioned, policing by consent, begins to fail, and no amount of equipment or legal authority can fully replace it."}
{"id": "cur-essay-518", "group": "gb-essay-518.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the influence of religion on the development of art during the Renaissance and Baroque periods, using specific examples such as Michelangelo's Sistine Chapel Ceiling, Grünewald's Isenheim Altarpiece, and Caravaggio's The Calling of St. Matthew. How did these works reflect religious motifs and subtexts, and what impact did they have on the viewers' perception of faith?", "text": "Religion was the principal patron, subject, and audience of European art between the fifteenth and seventeenth centuries. The Church commissioned most major works, and artists were expected to make doctrine visible to congregations who often could not read. Yet the way faith appeared in paint changed markedly from the High Renaissance to the Baroque, and three works show that shift clearly.\n\nMichelangelo's Sistine Chapel ceiling, painted between 1508 and 1512 for Pope Julius II, tells the story of Genesis through idealized, muscular bodies borrowed from classical sculpture. In the Creation of Adam, the nearly touching fingers of God and man suggest that humanity is made in the divine image and possesses real dignity. The surrounding prophets and sibyls imply that both Hebrew scripture and pagan antiquity anticipated Christ. The ceiling reflects Renaissance humanism, in which faith and human grandeur reinforce each other, and the viewer looking up is invited to feel awe at an ordered, purposeful creation.\n\nGrünewald's Isenheim Altarpiece, completed around 1515, speaks in a very different voice. It was made for a monastery hospital whose monks cared for victims of ergotism, a disease that ravaged the skin and limbs. Christ's crucified body is greenish, twisted, and covered in sores, so patients could recognize their own suffering in his. When the panels were opened, a radiant Resurrection promised release from that pain. Here the religious meaning is bodily and consoling rather than intellectual.\n\nCaravaggio's Calling of St. Matthew, painted around 1600, belongs to the Counter-Reformation, when a Church challenged by Protestantism wanted art that was clear, emotional, and persuasive. Caravaggio sets the scene in what looks like a dim Roman counting room, with tax collectors in contemporary dress. A shaft of light follows Christ's gesture toward Matthew, and Christ's hand deliberately echoes that of Michelangelo's Adam. Grace arrives abruptly, in ordinary life, among sinners.\n\nTogether these works moved viewers from admiration to empathy to personal confrontation. Michelangelo showed believers the majesty of God's plan, Grünewald assured them that God shared their agony, and Caravaggio suggested that the call to faith could reach anyone in the middle of an unremarkable day."}
{"id": "cur-essay-746", "group": "gb-essay-746.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "their value system and cultural beliefs regarding nature. This can help create a better understanding and mutual respect between the two cultures, leading to more effective collaboration in preserving the remaining forests.\n  * Another alternative is implementing policies that incorporate both Western conservation practices and the cultural beliefs of the Lummi people. This can be done through joint decision-making processes, where the tribe representatives and government organizations work together to find solutions that respect both perspectives.\n  * Additionally, education and awareness programs can be implemented to foster a better understanding of different cultural perspectives on nature. This can help bridge the gap between the Western and Native American civilizations and promote more harmonious relationships with nature.\n\nDiscuss the discrepancies between the cultural value systems of Western civilization and Native American tribes, using the case study of the Lummi people in Washington state. Explore potential alternatives for improving understanding and collaboration between these cultures in preserving nature.", "text": "The dispute over old-growth forests in Washington state illustrates how deeply two cultures can differ in what they believe nature is for. The Lummi, a Coast Salish people whose homeland lies around the northern Puget Sound, have traditionally understood the forest as a living community to which they belong. Ancient cedars supply material for canoes, houses, and ceremonial objects, but particular groves are also places of spiritual training, where individuals seek guidance and where the presence of ancestors is felt. A site cannot simply be exchanged for another, because its meaning is tied to that exact place and to its undisturbed condition.\n\nThe dominant Western tradition has tended to see the same trees differently. Shaped by ideas of private property, economic progress, and a separation between humans and the natural world, it treats forests primarily as resources whose value can be measured in board feet, jobs, and revenue. Even Western conservation often relies on this logic, defending wilderness for recreation, biodiversity, or future use. Both approaches can protect trees, but neither fully captures the Lummi claim that the land holds obligations, not just benefits. When agencies weigh sacred sites against timber sales in a cost-benefit analysis, the two sides are not really speaking the same language.\n\nSeveral alternatives could narrow this gap. The first is sustained dialogue in which officials and timber interests listen to Lummi elders explain their beliefs on their own terms, rather than translating them into economic categories. The second is shared authority. Co-management agreements, already used for salmon fisheries in the region, give tribal representatives a real vote in decisions instead of a consultative role that can be ignored. Treaty rights provide a legal basis for such arrangements. The third is education, including school curricula and public programs that present Indigenous ecological knowledge as a serious body of understanding built over thousands of years.\n\nNone of these measures erases the underlying difference in values, and they should not try to. Their purpose is to create enough mutual respect that the remaining old-growth forests are governed by people who understand both what the trees are worth and what they mean."}
{"id": "cur-essay-222", "group": "gb-essay-222.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the harmful effects of electronic nicotine delivery systems (e-cigarettes) on the human body and argue for the regulation and restriction of these products to protect public health.", "text": "Electronic cigarettes were introduced as a cleaner alternative to smoking, and for adults who switch completely from combustible tobacco they probably do reduce exposure to some toxins. That narrow benefit has been used to obscure a broader problem. E-cigarettes are not harmless, they have drawn millions of young people into nicotine dependence, and they deserve far stricter regulation than they currently receive in many places.\n\nThe most certain harm is addiction. Nicotine salts used in modern devices deliver high doses smoothly, and a single pod can contain as much nicotine as a pack of cigarettes. In adolescents and young adults, whose brains are still developing, nicotine exposure is associated with problems in attention, mood, and impulse control, and it increases the likelihood of later smoking. Nicotine also raises heart rate and blood pressure and stiffens arteries, which places strain on the cardiovascular system.\n\nThe aerosol itself carries additional risks. Heating the liquid produces formaldehyde, acrolein, and other irritants, along with fine particles and traces of metals such as nickel and lead from the heating coil. Users report coughing, wheezing, and worsened asthma, and laboratory studies show inflammation and impaired immune defenses in the airways. The 2019 outbreak of severe lung injury in the United States, linked mainly to vitamin E acetate in illicit cannabis cartridges, showed how quickly an unregulated product can hospitalize thousands. The long-term effects of years of vaping remain unknown simply because the products are too new.\n\nGiven this evidence, regulation is a reasonable precaution. Governments should restrict the sweet and fruit flavors that appeal most strongly to teenagers, enforce age verification for both store and online sales, and limit advertising on social media. Nicotine concentrations should be capped, ingredients should be disclosed and tested, and disposable devices, which are cheap and easy to hide, merit particular scrutiny. Taxes can discourage youth uptake while keeping vaping less expensive than smoking for adults trying to quit.\n\nPublic health policy learned slowly and painfully with cigarettes. With e-cigarettes, there is an opportunity to act before another generation discovers the consequences decades too late."}
{"id": "cur-essay-330", "group": "gb-essay-330.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "\"Discuss the steps and materials needed to change a hard disk drive (HDD) in a computer, and explain why it is important to seek assistance for those with little knowledge of computer hardware.\"", "text": "Replacing a hard disk drive is one of the more approachable computer repairs, but it still requires preparation, the right materials, and care. The essential items are a compatible replacement drive, a Phillips screwdriver, an antistatic wrist strap or at least a grounded metal surface to touch, and a clean, well-lit workspace. It is also wise to have an external drive or cloud storage for backups, a spare SATA data cable, and either cloning software or a USB installer for the operating system. Before buying the new drive, the owner should confirm its physical size, usually 3.5 inches for desktops and 2.5 inches for laptops, and its interface.\n\nThe first step is to back up every important file, since the old drive may be failing or may not be readable later. Next, shut the computer down, unplug the power cable, and, on a laptop, remove the battery if possible. Pressing the power button for a few seconds discharges any remaining electricity. After grounding yourself, open the case or the access panel, locate the drive, and disconnect its power and data cables by pulling on the connectors, not the wires. Remove the screws or release the bracket holding the drive, slide it out, and fit the new one in the same position. Reattach the cables firmly, secure the drive, and close the case. When the computer is powered on, check that the firmware detects the drive, then install the operating system or restore the cloned image and copy the backed-up files into place.\n\nAlthough the procedure sounds simple, people with little hardware knowledge should seek assistance. Static electricity can silently destroy components, connectors and ribbon cables in laptops break easily, and a forgotten backup can mean permanent loss of photographs and documents. An inexperienced person may also buy an incompatible drive, void a warranty by opening a sealed case, or become stuck when the system does not boot. A knowledgeable friend or a technician can complete the work quickly, verify that data has transferred correctly, and dispose of the old drive securely. The modest cost of help is small compared with the cost of a damaged machine or lost information."}
{"id": "cur-essay-77", "group": "gb-essay-77.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Explore the origins, beliefs, and ethical principles of Jainism and discuss the significance of its rejection of a Creator or God, the emphasis on non-violence, and the potential controversy surrounding its worldview in relation to other religions and philosophies.", "text": "Jainism is one of the oldest living religions of India, emerging from the same climate of ascetic questioning that produced Buddhism. Its followers regard it as eternal, taught anew in each age by a line of twenty-four tirthankaras, or \"ford-makers,\" who show the way across the river of rebirth. Historians usually associate its present form with Mahavira, a contemporary of the Buddha in roughly the sixth century BCE, and with his predecessor Parshvanatha. Mahavira renounced a princely life, practiced severe austerities, and is said to have attained complete knowledge.\n\nJain teaching divides reality into souls and non-soul substances. Every living thing, from humans to insects and even beings in water and earth, possesses a soul capable of perfect knowledge and bliss. Karma is understood as a subtle material that clings to the soul because of passion and harmful action, weighing it down through successive lives. Liberation comes by stopping the inflow of karma and burning off what remains through right faith, right knowledge, and right conduct. Conduct is defined by five vows: non-violence, truthfulness, not stealing, sexual restraint, and non-attachment to possessions.\n\nTwo features give the tradition its distinctive character. First, Jainism denies a creator God. The universe is uncreated and governed by its own laws, and liberated souls are honored as examples, not as rulers who grant favors. Responsibility for salvation therefore rests entirely with the individual. Second, non-violence is carried further than in almost any other ethic. Jains are vegetarian, many avoid root vegetables, and monks may sweep the path before them to spare small creatures. The related doctrine of many-sidedness holds that truth is complex and no single viewpoint captures it, which encourages intellectual humility.\n\nThese positions have drawn criticism. Theistic traditions have called Jainism atheistic, and Hindu thinkers objected to its rejection of Vedic authority and sacrifice. Others find its asceticism extreme, particularly the practice of fasting to death at the end of life, which Indian courts have debated. Critics also argue that perfect non-violence is impossible in practice. Even so, Jain ideas shaped Gandhi's politics and continue to inform modern discussions of animal welfare and environmental restraint."}
{"id": "cur-essay-639", "group": "gb-essay-639.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the rise and fall of the Enron Corporation, including the circumstances and factors that affected its struggle. Evaluate the fatal mistakes made by the corporation and analyze theories and assumptions about the reasons behind its bankruptcy.", "text": "Enron began in 1985 as the product of a merger between two natural gas pipeline companies, and under chairman Kenneth Lay it grew into what appeared to be one of the most innovative firms in America. As energy markets were deregulated, Enron shifted from owning pipelines to trading contracts for gas and electricity, and later for broadband capacity, weather risk, and almost anything else that could be priced. By 2000 it reported revenues above 100 billion dollars and its shares traded near 90 dollars. In December 2001 it filed for what was then the largest bankruptcy in American history.\n\nThe collapse had several causes that reinforced one another. Jeffrey Skilling persuaded regulators to let Enron use mark-to-market accounting, which allowed the company to book the estimated lifetime profit of a long-term deal on the day it was signed. Earnings therefore depended on optimistic forecasts rather than cash, and each quarter demanded new deals to keep growth alive. When investments in water, broadband, and overseas power plants performed poorly, chief financial officer Andrew Fastow created special purpose entities that kept debt and losses off the balance sheet. These partnerships were backed largely by Enron's own stock, so a falling share price threatened the whole structure.\n\nThe fatal mistakes were as much cultural as technical. A ranking system that dismissed the lowest-rated employees each year rewarded aggressive deal-making and discouraged dissent. Executives were paid heavily in stock options, which tied their wealth to short-term prices. The board waived its own conflict-of-interest rules for Fastow, and the auditor, Arthur Andersen, earned large consulting fees from the client it was supposed to scrutinize.\n\nScholars explain the failure in different ways. Agency theory emphasizes managers enriching themselves at shareholders' expense. Others stress the failure of gatekeepers such as auditors, analysts, banks, and rating agencies, all of whom had incentives to stay quiet. A third view holds that the business model itself was weaker than it looked and that fraud merely delayed recognition. Most likely all three are true. The lasting result was the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 and a persistent lesson about what happens when reported numbers drift away from reality."}
{"id": "cur-essay-959", "group": "gb-essay-959.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "\"Examine the impact of dietary choices on health and lifestyle, comparing the perspectives and practices of herb-eaters and meat-eaters.\"", "text": "What people choose to eat shapes their health, their daily routines, and often their sense of identity. The contrast between those who eat only plants and those who include meat is one of the clearest examples. A fair comparison shows that both patterns can support a healthy life, but they carry different strengths and risks.\n\nPeople who follow vegetarian or vegan diets generally consume more fiber, fruit, vegetables, legumes, and whole grains, and less saturated fat. Large studies associate these diets with lower body weight, lower blood pressure and cholesterol, and reduced rates of heart disease and type 2 diabetes. Many plant-eaters are also motivated by concern for animals or for the environment, since raising livestock demands more land and water and produces more greenhouse gases than growing crops. Their lifestyle often involves careful label reading, more home cooking, and some planning in restaurants or while traveling. The main nutritional challenge is obtaining enough vitamin B12, iron, zinc, calcium, and omega-3 fatty acids, which may require fortified foods or supplements.\n\nMeat-eaters point to different advantages. Meat, fish, eggs, and dairy supply complete protein and highly absorbable iron and B12 in small portions, which is valuable for growing children, pregnant women, athletes, and older adults at risk of muscle loss. Meat is also central to many cuisines and family traditions, and an omnivorous diet is convenient almost everywhere. The risks come mainly from quantity and type. High intake of processed and red meat is linked to colorectal cancer and cardiovascular disease, and a plate dominated by meat can crowd out vegetables.\n\nThe two groups are less opposed than their arguments suggest. A vegetarian living on refined starch, sugar, and fried snacks is not eating well, and an omnivore who favors fish, poultry, and plenty of plants may be very healthy. Other habits, such as exercise, sleep, smoking, and alcohol use, also explain part of the differences seen in studies. The most reliable conclusion is that the quality and balance of a diet matter more than its label, and that thoughtful eaters of either kind have more in common than they often admit."}
{"id": "cur-essay-648", "group": "gb-essay-648.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the portrayal of women in film using the concept of the male gaze theory and psychoanalysis. Analyze the limitations that the family places on women in the melodrama genre and how it reinforces the dominance-submission patterns between men and women. Provide specific examples from the films \"The Postman Always Rings Twice\" and \"The Dark Knight Rises\" to support your argument.", "text": "Laura Mulvey's 1975 essay \"Visual Pleasure and Narrative Cinema\" argued that classical Hollywood film is organized around a male gaze. Drawing on Freud and Lacan, she claimed that cinema offers the pleasure of looking at another person as an object and the pleasure of identifying with an idealized figure on screen. Because the active figure is typically male, the camera, the hero, and the spectator share one point of view, while the woman is displayed as spectacle. Her image is pleasurable but also threatening, and films manage that threat either by investigating and punishing her or by turning her into a glamorous fetish.\n\nThe Postman Always Rings Twice (1946) follows this pattern almost exactly. Cora is introduced through Frank's eyes: a lipstick rolls across the floor, and the camera travels from her legs upward to reveal her posed in white in the doorway. From that moment she is defined by the desire she provokes. Yet her own wishes are modest and domestic. She wants to make something of the roadside diner and to escape a marriage to an older man she does not love. The household is her cage, since her husband can decide to sell the business and move her to Canada to nurse his sister without consulting her. Her only route to independence runs through another man, and the narrative punishes her ambition with death.\n\nThe Dark Knight Rises (2012) seems to offer stronger women, but similar structures remain. Selina Kyle is skilled and cynical, yet the film repeatedly frames her body in a tight suit, and her story ends when she becomes Bruce Wayne's companion, glimpsed at a café table through Alfred's approving eyes. Miranda Tate, revealed as Talia, acts entirely to complete her father's mission. Her power turns out to be a daughter's loyalty.\n\nIn both films, family in the melodramatic sense, whether marriage, inheritance, or romantic coupling, is the institution that contains female desire. Women may be dangerous for a time, but the plot restores a pattern in which men act and decide while women are watched, claimed, or eliminated. Mulvey's theory has limits, but these examples show why it endures."}
{"id": "cur-essay-286", "group": "gb-essay-286.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the benefits and challenges of international diversification for companies. Consider the expansion of market opportunities, economies of scale and learning, and the advantages of new locations. Additionally, explore the ethical implications and risks associated with globalization, particularly in emerging markets. Include examples from developing countries such as China, India, and Taiwan. Finally, examine the factors that may influence a company's decision to reject international expansion, such as reluctance to face globalization risks or a lack of understanding of foreign markets.", "text": "International diversification, the expansion of a company's sales or operations across national borders, offers some of the largest growth opportunities available to modern firms. The most obvious benefit is access to new customers. When a domestic market matures, countries with rising incomes can supply demand for decades. Starbucks, for example, has opened thousands of stores in China, and Walmart entered India's fast-growing retail sector by acquiring the e-commerce company Flipkart.\n\nA second benefit is scale and learning. Selling in many countries lets a firm spread research, design, and brand costs over greater volume, lowering the cost per unit. Operating abroad also exposes managers to new ideas and competitors. Multinationals have established research centers in Bangalore to draw on India's engineering talent and have learned from the speed of Chinese digital commerce. Location itself can be an advantage. Taiwan's concentration of semiconductor expertise, led by TSMC, attracts chip designers from around the world, and China's dense supplier networks allow electronics to be assembled quickly and cheaply.\n\nThese advantages come with serious risks. Governments may change regulations, impose tariffs, or restrict foreign ownership, and tensions between China and Taiwan show how politics can threaten an entire supply chain. Currency swings can erase profits, and weak protection of intellectual property can create local rivals. Ethical problems are equally important. Low costs in emerging markets sometimes rest on long hours, unsafe conditions, or environmental damage, as criticism of labor practices at large electronics factories in China has shown. Companies may also face pressure to pay bribes or to cooperate with censorship. A firm that ignores these issues risks harming people as well as its own reputation.\n\nFor these reasons some companies decline to expand. Managers may judge that political and financial risks outweigh the potential returns, or they may recognize that they do not understand foreign consumers, laws, and business customs well enough to compete. Coordinating distant operations is costly, and well-known retailers have withdrawn from markets after misreading local tastes.\n\nInternational expansion therefore rewards preparation. Companies that study markets carefully, enter gradually, and hold themselves to consistent ethical standards are the ones most likely to gain."}
{"id": "cur-essay-688", "group": "gb-essay-688.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "What role can the World Summit on Information Society (WSIS) and civil society organizations play in addressing socioeconomic inequities in post-colonial communities, and what practical control tools should be implemented to ensure ethical and diverse actions?", "text": "The World Summit on the Information Society, held in Geneva in 2003 and Tunis in 2005 under United Nations auspices, set out the goal of a people-centred, inclusive information society. For communities that carry the legacy of colonial rule, that goal is far from abstract. Colonial economies were built to extract resources and send value elsewhere, and digital infrastructure can repeat the pattern when cables, platforms, and data centres are owned abroad, when local languages are absent online, and when data about a population is collected and monetized by distant firms.\n\nWSIS matters because it is one of the few global processes in which governments, companies, technical bodies, and civil society deliberate together. Its action lines on access, capacity building, cultural and linguistic diversity, and the ethical dimensions of information provide commitments that can be cited when holding states and corporations to account. The Internet Governance Forum that emerged from it gives smaller countries and community groups a platform they would not otherwise have. Its weakness is that outcomes are non-binding, and participation still favours those who can afford to travel and lobby.\n\nCivil society organizations fill part of this gap. They build community networks where commercial providers see no profit, teach digital skills, document harms, and translate global principles into local demands. They are also able to challenge projects that arrive labelled as development but mainly serve outside interests. Their legitimacy, however, depends on being rooted in the communities they claim to represent, not only in international funding circles.\n\nPractical control tools are needed to keep these efforts ethical and diverse. Projects should require community consent and a published impact assessment before deployment. Funding and procurement should be transparent, with independent audits and public reporting against indicators broken down by gender, income, language, and region. Governance boards should reserve seats for local representatives, including women and Indigenous groups. Data protection rules should recognize community ownership of data, and requirements for local-language content and open standards should be written into contracts. Finally, accessible complaint mechanisms should allow residents to contest decisions.\n\nWith such safeguards, WSIS and civil society can help digital development redistribute opportunity instead of reproducing old hierarchies."}
{"id": "cur-essay-916", "group": "gb-essay-916.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the increasing role of diasporas in the global economy and how their economic potential has become comparable, and in some cases, even exceeding the potential of the ethnic groups forming the environment.", "text": "Diasporas, communities living outside their ancestral homelands while keeping ties to them, have become significant actors in the global economy. Migration, cheap communication, and easier movement of capital have turned what were once scattered minorities into transnational networks that trade, invest, and transfer knowledge across borders. In some cases their economic weight now rivals or exceeds that of the majority populations among whom they live.\n\nThe clearest measure of this influence is remittances. Migrants send home hundreds of billions of dollars each year, and for low- and middle-income countries as a group these transfers now exceed foreign direct investment and are several times larger than official aid. In countries such as Tajikistan, Nepal, Lebanon, and Tonga they make up a large fraction of national income. Diasporas also invest directly. Overseas Chinese entrepreneurs supplied much of the early foreign capital that financed China's manufacturing boom, and Indian engineers returning from Silicon Valley helped build the software industry in Bangalore and Hyderabad.\n\nWithin host societies, some diaspora groups hold a disproportionate share of wealth. Ethnic Chinese minorities in Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand, and the Philippines are a small part of the population but control a large part of private business. Indian Americans have the highest median household income of any major ethnic group in the United States, and Lebanese, Armenian, and Jewish communities have long been prominent in commerce and finance across several continents.\n\nSeveral factors explain this performance. Migrants are often self-selected for ambition and education. Shared language and kinship create trust, which lowers the cost of doing business across borders where formal institutions are weak. Exclusion from land ownership or public employment historically pushed many minorities into trade and the professions, and their position between cultures lets them act as intermediaries.\n\nThis success carries complications. Economic prominence has sometimes provoked resentment and violence, as in the expulsion of Asians from Uganda in 1972. Emigration can drain talent from home countries, and averages conceal many poor migrants. Governments nevertheless increasingly court their diasporas with dual citizenship, special bonds, and investment incentives. That attention reflects a real shift: economic power today flows through networks of people as much as through territories."}
{"id": "cur-essay-335", "group": "gb-essay-335.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "How has IKEA successfully implemented the components of the marketing mix to establish its global success? Discuss the company's pricing strategy, promotional techniques, accessibility of locations, and innovative product offerings in relation to its achievements in the market.", "text": "IKEA has grown from a small Swedish mail-order business founded by Ingvar Kamprad in 1943 into the world's largest furniture retailer, and its use of the marketing mix explains much of that success. Each of the four elements, price, promotion, place, and product, supports a single idea: offering well-designed home furnishings at prices most people can afford.\n\nPricing is the starting point, not the end, of IKEA's process. The company often decides what a product should cost and then asks designers and suppliers to meet that figure. Flat packaging reduces shipping and storage costs, customers transport and assemble furniture themselves, and enormous order volumes give IKEA bargaining power with manufacturers. The savings are passed on, and prices for popular items have frequently been lowered over time. In markets such as China, IKEA cut prices substantially and sourced locally to reach ordinary households.\n\nPromotion reinforces this positioning with a distinctive, friendly tone. For decades the printed catalogue, distributed in the hundreds of millions, was the centrepiece, showing complete rooms instead of isolated products. Today the company relies more on its website, social media, a loyalty club, and an augmented reality app that lets customers preview furniture at home. Humorous advertising and in-store room displays invite shoppers to imagine a better everyday life.\n\nPlace has been equally deliberate. The classic IKEA store is a large blue and yellow building on the edge of a city, with ample parking, a one-way path through the showroom, a self-service warehouse, and a restaurant that encourages families to stay for hours. As more people live in dense cities and shop online, IKEA has added smaller urban stores, planning studios, delivery services, and e-commerce.\n\nProduct innovation ties the mix together. The range combines simple Scandinavian design with function, quality, sustainability, and low price, an approach the company calls democratic design. Items such as the Billy bookcase have sold in the tens of millions, and the catalogue of products is adapted modestly to local homes, for example with different bed sizes and kitchen layouts.\n\nBecause every element points in the same direction, customers understand what IKEA stands for, and competitors have found the whole system difficult to copy."}
{"id": "cur-essay-738", "group": "gb-essay-738.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the significance of a scientific question in understanding the components of matter and how it has influenced human development and the world as we know it today.", "text": "Few questions have shaped civilization as much as a simple one: what is everything made of? It sounds like a child's question, yet the attempt to answer it has driven more than two thousand years of inquiry and has produced much of the technology that defines modern life.\n\nThe earliest answers were philosophical. Greek thinkers proposed that matter was built from a few elements, such as earth, water, air, and fire, while Democritus suggested that it consisted of tiny, indivisible particles moving in empty space. Without experiments, these ideas could not be tested. That changed in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, when chemists began weighing substances carefully. Lavoisier showed that mass is conserved in reactions, Dalton argued that each element consists of identical atoms that combine in fixed ratios, and Mendeleev arranged the elements in a periodic table that predicted undiscovered ones.\n\nThe question then moved inside the atom. Thomson identified the electron in 1897, Rutherford discovered the dense nucleus in 1911, and Bohr and the founders of quantum mechanics explained how electrons occupy specific energy levels. Later work revealed protons, neutrons, and eventually quarks, and the detection of the Higgs boson in 2012 completed the Standard Model of particle physics. Each answer raised a deeper version of the original question, and that pattern continues with unsolved puzzles such as dark matter.\n\nThe practical consequences have been enormous. Understanding atoms and bonds made modern chemistry possible, giving us fertilizers that feed billions, plastics, synthetic fibers, and pharmaceuticals. Knowledge of electrons led to electricity distribution, and quantum theory led to the transistor, the laser, and every computer and phone. Nuclear physics produced both power plants and weapons, changing energy policy and international politics. Medical imaging, from X-rays to MRI and PET scans, depends directly on the physics of matter.\n\nThe question also changed how humans think. It demonstrated that nature follows consistent laws that can be discovered through hypothesis and experiment, and that invisible structures can explain visible properties. In that sense its greatest contribution is the scientific method it helped refine. The world we live in, materially and intellectually, is largely an extended answer to it."}
{"id": "cur-essay-14", "group": "gb-essay-14.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "What is the significance of the ADRB1 gene mutation and its impact on sleep patterns in humans?", "text": "Most adults need between seven and nine hours of sleep to function well, yet a small number of people feel fully rested after far less. In 2019, researchers at the University of California, San Francisco, led by Ying-Hui Fu and Louis Ptáček, reported that a rare mutation in the ADRB1 gene helps explain this trait, known as familial natural short sleep. The finding matters because it links a specific molecular change to how much sleep a person requires.\n\nADRB1 encodes the beta-1 adrenergic receptor, a protein that responds to noradrenaline and is best known for its role in regulating the heart. The researchers studied a family in which several members habitually slept only about four to six hours a night without apparent tiredness or health problems. Those individuals carried a single change in the gene that swaps one amino acid for another and makes the receptor less stable. To test whether the mutation caused the trait, the team engineered mice with the same variant. The mice slept roughly an hour less per day than normal animals.\n\nFurther experiments pointed to a mechanism. Neurons that express the receptor are abundant in the dorsal pons, a region of the brainstem involved in controlling sleep states. These cells are active during wakefulness and REM sleep and quiet during deep sleep. Stimulating them woke sleeping mice, and in mutant animals the neurons were more easily excited, which suggests that the mutation tilts the brain toward wakefulness.\n\nThe significance of this work is broader than one family. Together with earlier discoveries in genes such as DEC2, it shows that sleep duration is partly under genetic control and that some people may sleep more efficiently. Understanding how they do so could eventually guide treatments for insomnia and other sleep disorders, or reveal how the restorative work of sleep is accomplished.\n\nCaution is still necessary. The mutation is extremely rare, the original findings come from a small number of people and from mice, and long-term health effects are not fully known. Most people who sleep five hours a night are not natural short sleepers. They are simply sleep deprived, with the well-documented risks that follow."}
{"id": "cur-essay-293", "group": "gb-essay-293.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Analyzing the Significance of Bulls in South Sudan: A Review of Grey Bull", "text": "In South Sudan, cattle are far more than livestock. Among pastoral peoples such as the Dinka, Nuer, and Mundari, bulls and oxen stand at the centre of economic, social, and spiritual life, and the short film Grey Bull uses that fact to tell a story about displacement and identity. The film follows a South Sudanese man who has resettled in Australia and works in an abattoir, where his encounter with a grey bull destined for slaughter forces him to confront what he has left behind. To understand why the animal affects him so deeply, a viewer needs to understand what a bull means in the world he came from.\n\nCattle are the traditional measure of wealth and the currency of the most important transactions. Marriage is sealed through bridewealth paid in cattle, which binds families together, and disputes are settled by compensation in animals. Milk is a staple food, dung fires keep insects away from the camps, and ash protects the skin. Beyond these uses, a young man is given a particular ox at his initiation, and he takes a name derived from its colour and markings. He composes songs praising it and shapes its horns. To share a name with an animal is to see it as an extension of oneself.\n\nGrey Bull draws its emotional force from this bond. In the slaughterhouse, cattle are anonymous units of meat. For the protagonist, a grey bull is a namesake and a living link to his youth, his family, and his homeland. The collision between those two ways of seeing the same animal expresses the wider experience of refugees who must earn a living inside a culture that does not recognize what they value. His response to the bull becomes a way of holding on to himself.\n\nThe film also reminds viewers that South Sudanese culture is not defined only by war. Cattle have been both a cause of raiding and a source of beauty, poetry, and belonging. By concentrating on one man and one animal, Grey Bull shows how an entire system of meaning can travel across the world and survive in memory."}
{"id": "cur-essay-716", "group": "gb-essay-716.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Write an essay analyzing the ethical implications of using drones in warfare and the decision-making process behind ethical egoism, existentialism, and hedonism.", "text": "Armed drones allow a state to kill an enemy thousands of miles away while its own operator sits in safety. That asymmetry is the source of both their appeal and the ethical unease they provoke. Supporters argue that drones are more precise than conventional bombing and spare the lives of soldiers. Critics respond that removing risk makes war too easy to begin and continue, that civilian casualties are often undercounted, that strikes outside declared battlefields weaken international law, and that distance may dull the moral seriousness of killing. Looking at the question through ethical egoism, existentialism, and hedonism shows how differently a decision to use drones can be reasoned.\n\nEthical egoism holds that an agent ought to act in its own interest. Applied to a state, it seems to endorse drones readily, since they protect one's own personnel, cost less than deploying troops, and avoid political backlash at home. A thoughtful egoist, however, must weigh long-term interest. Strikes that kill civilians can generate resentment, aid enemy recruitment, and set precedents that rivals will later use. Even on purely self-interested grounds, the calculation is less simple than it first appears.\n\nExistentialism shifts attention from outcomes to responsibility. For Sartre, people are condemned to be free and cannot hide behind orders, roles, or technology without falling into bad faith. A drone operator, a commander, or a president who says the system made the decision is denying personal authorship of the act. Existentialism does not forbid killing in war, but it insists that whoever chooses must own the choice fully and recognize that in choosing, one affirms a kind of world for everyone.\n\nHedonism judges actions by pleasure and pain. An egoistic hedonist might favour drones because they minimize suffering for one's own side. A universal hedonist, close to utilitarianism, would count the fear of populations living under constant surveillance and the grief of bereaved families alongside any harm prevented.\n\nNone of these theories settles the matter alone. Together they suggest that drones are defensible only when decision-makers look beyond immediate advantage, count all who suffer, and accept responsibility without evasion."}
{"id": "cur-essay-962", "group": "gb-essay-962.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the different capabilities and uses of three popular digital forensics tools: Autopsy, Arsenal Recon, and Cellebrite UFED. Compare their features, availability, and cost, and explain why each tool is valuable for conducting digital forensic investigations.", "text": "Digital forensic investigators rarely depend on a single program. Different tools specialize in different kinds of evidence, and three widely used examples, Autopsy, Arsenal Recon, and Cellebrite UFED, show how they complement one another.\n\nAutopsy is an open-source platform that provides a graphical interface to The Sleuth Kit. It analyzes disk images and storage media from computers and, to a lesser extent, phones. Its modules recover deleted files, extract web history and email, search by keyword, compare files against hash sets of known material, and assemble activity into a timeline. Users can add their own plug-ins written in Python or Java. Because it is free to download, Autopsy is common in universities, small agencies, and corporate teams with limited budgets, and since its source code is public, its methods can be examined when results are challenged in court. Its limitations are slower processing on very large cases and weaker support for modern smartphones.\n\nArsenal Recon produces a suite of specialized Windows forensics tools. The best known, Arsenal Image Mounter, mounts a forensic image so that Windows treats it as a real physical disk, which allows examiners to work with encrypted volumes and shadow copies and even to boot the image as a virtual machine. Other tools in the suite rebuild historical versions of the Windows Registry and extract data from hibernation files. The Image Mounter has a free mode, while full functionality requires a paid subscription that is modest compared with large commercial suites. Its value lies in depth, revealing artifacts that general-purpose tools miss.\n\nCellebrite UFED focuses on mobile devices. It performs logical, file system, and physical extractions from thousands of phone and tablet models, can bypass many screen locks, and recovers messages, call logs, location data, and application content, including deleted items. It is sold as dedicated hardware or as software, mainly to law enforcement and vetted organizations, and licences cost many thousands of dollars per year.\n\nThe three tools therefore occupy different positions. Autopsy offers accessible, transparent general analysis, Arsenal provides precise examination of Windows systems, and Cellebrite opens the phones on which much modern evidence resides. A well-equipped laboratory benefits from all three."}
{"id": "cur-essay-646", "group": "gb-essay-646.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the fundamental traits of a philosopher, including open-mindedness, skepticism, and a systemic thinking approach. Explain how these traits contribute to the development of critical thinking skills and their importance in higher education.", "text": "A philosopher is defined less by a body of knowledge than by a way of thinking. Three traits in particular, open-mindedness, skepticism, and a systematic approach to ideas, characterize philosophical inquiry, and together they form the basis of the critical thinking that higher education aims to develop.\n\nOpen-mindedness is the willingness to take seriously ideas that differ from one's own. It does not mean accepting every view as equally valid. It means giving an argument a fair hearing, trying to state an opponent's position in its strongest form, and being prepared to change one's mind when the reasons are good. Socrates modeled this attitude by claiming that his wisdom lay in recognizing how much he did not know. A student who approaches a text or a classmate with that humility learns far more than one who looks only for confirmation.\n\nSkepticism balances openness. The philosopher does not accept a claim because it is popular, traditional, or stated by an authority, but asks what evidence supports it and whether the reasoning holds. Descartes pushed this habit to its limit by doubting everything he could. Healthy skepticism is not cynicism. Its purpose is to proportion belief to evidence, and it applies to one's own assumptions as much as to those of others.\n\nSystematic thinking connects individual judgments into a coherent whole. Philosophers define their terms, lay out premises in order, trace the implications of a position, and check whether their beliefs about one subject are consistent with their beliefs about another. This discipline exposes hidden contradictions and shows how a small assumption can carry large consequences.\n\nCombined, these traits are critical thinking in practice. Openness supplies alternatives, skepticism tests them, and systematic reasoning organizes what survives. Higher education depends on precisely these abilities. University students are expected to evaluate sources, construct arguments, and solve problems that have no answer key, and they will enter workplaces and a public life crowded with persuasive but unreliable information. Facts learned for an examination fade or become outdated, but the habits of questioning carefully, listening honestly, and reasoning in an orderly way remain useful in every field and throughout life."}
{"id": "cur-essay-35", "group": "gb-essay-35.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Prompt:\n\nDiscuss the pathophysiology, symptoms, and treatment options for allergic rhinitis. Explain how the hypersensitivity reaction plays a role in the development of this condition and describe the specific inflammatory mediators involved. Explore the subjective and objective conclusions that can be drawn from a patient's symptoms. Finally, evaluate the effectiveness of pharmacological treatments, such as intranasal antihistamines, and the importance of environmental control and nasal irrigation in managing allergic rhinitis.", "text": "Allergic rhinitis is an inflammatory disorder of the nasal mucosa caused by an immune response to otherwise harmless airborne substances such as pollen, dust mites, animal dander, and mold. It is a classic type I hypersensitivity reaction. During initial exposure, antigen-presenting cells process the allergen and stimulate T helper 2 lymphocytes, which release interleukin-4 and interleukin-13. These cytokines drive B cells to produce allergen-specific IgE, which binds to receptors on mast cells and basophils. The person is now sensitized.\n\nOn re-exposure, the allergen cross-links IgE on mast cells and triggers degranulation within minutes. Histamine is the principal preformed mediator, producing itching, sneezing, vasodilation, and watery discharge. Mast cells also release tryptase and synthesize cysteinyl leukotrienes and prostaglandin D2, which increase vascular permeability and congestion. Several hours later a late-phase response follows, in which interleukin-5 and chemokines recruit eosinophils and other cells that sustain inflammation and make the nose more reactive to further exposure.\n\nAssessment combines subjective and objective findings. Patients describe sneezing fits, an itchy nose, palate, or eyes, clear rhinorrhea, nasal blockage, postnasal drip, fatigue, and poor sleep, often with a seasonal pattern or a clear trigger. On examination, the clinician may see pale, swollen turbinates, clear secretions, dark circles under the eyes, a transverse nasal crease from repeated rubbing, and cobblestoning of the posterior pharynx. Skin prick testing or serum-specific IgE confirms the responsible allergens.\n\nTreatment is stepwise. Intranasal corticosteroids are the most effective single therapy for persistent symptoms, particularly congestion. Intranasal antihistamines such as azelastine act within minutes, relieve congestion better than oral antihistamines, and work well in combination with a steroid spray, although some patients dislike the bitter taste. Second-generation oral antihistamines are convenient for mild disease, and allergen immunotherapy can alter the underlying immune response in selected patients.\n\nNon-pharmacological measures remain important. Reducing exposure through mite-proof bedding, washing linens in hot water, keeping windows closed during high pollen counts, and limiting contact with pets lowers the allergen burden, although single measures alone often give modest results. Saline nasal irrigation, prepared with sterile or boiled water, clears allergens and mediators from the mucosa, is inexpensive and safe, and can reduce the amount of medication needed."}
{"id": "cur-essay-678", "group": "gb-essay-678.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the cultural significance and artistic value of the Nefertiti Bust as a representation of women's role in ancient Egyptian politics.", "text": "The painted limestone bust of Nefertiti, made around 1345 BCE and attributed to the workshop of the sculptor Thutmose at Amarna, is among the most recognized objects to survive from the ancient world. Discovered in 1912 by a German expedition and now displayed in Berlin, it is admired for its beauty, but it also offers evidence about the political position a royal woman could occupy in Egypt.\n\nAs a work of art, the bust is remarkable for its balance and refinement. The long neck, the forward tilt of the head, and the tall, flat-topped blue crown create a composition that seems both poised and alert. Layers of plaster over the limestone core allowed the sculptor to model the face with subtle realism, including fine lines around the mouth, while the symmetry gives it an idealized calm. The colours remain vivid, and the single inlaid eye of crystal lends the face an uncanny life. It was probably a master model for other portraits of the queen, which may explain its precision.\n\nIts cultural significance lies in what it communicates about Nefertiti's status. She was the principal wife of Akhenaten, the pharaoh who replaced Egypt's traditional gods with the worship of the sun disc, the Aten. In the art of his reign she appears with unusual prominence: making offerings to the Aten beside her husband, riding in her own chariot, and even striking down Egypt's enemies, a pose normally reserved for kings. The distinctive crown on the bust is hers alone and marks her as a figure of authority. Some scholars believe she ruled as co-regent or briefly as pharaoh after Akhenaten's death.\n\nThe bust therefore represents a tradition in which royal women could exercise real influence, as Hatshepsut and Queen Tiye had done earlier. Some caution is needed, since these women were exceptional, and their power derived from proximity to the king. The image is also an official ideal, not a candid likeness.\n\nToday the bust carries further meanings. It has become a global emblem of feminine beauty and of ancient Egypt itself, and Egypt's repeated requests for its return make it a focus of debate about who owns the past."}
{"id": "cur-essay-877", "group": "gb-essay-877.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "not beneficial or may even be harmful.\n\nHow has science influenced the field of psychology and how does psychology contribute to improving people's lives through interventions and therapies?", "text": "Psychology became a distinct discipline when it adopted the methods of science. Before the late nineteenth century, questions about the mind belonged mainly to philosophy and were answered through reflection and argument. When Wilhelm Wundt opened his laboratory in Leipzig in 1879, he began measuring mental processes under controlled conditions, and the field has been shaped by that commitment ever since.\n\nScience influenced psychology first through method. Researchers formulate hypotheses, design experiments with control groups, collect data systematically, and use statistics to judge whether results could have arisen by chance. Behaviorists such as Pavlov and Skinner applied this rigor to learning, and the cognitive revolution extended it to memory, attention, and reasoning. Advances in biology added further tools. Brain imaging, genetics, and pharmacology now allow psychologists to connect thoughts and emotions to neural activity and chemistry. Equally important is the scientific attitude of self-correction. Recent efforts to replicate famous studies, some of which failed, show the discipline testing its own conclusions.\n\nThis foundation is what allows psychology to improve lives responsibly. Treatments are evaluated in randomized controlled trials, so therapists can know which ones work. Cognitive behavioral therapy helps people with depression and anxiety identify and change unhelpful patterns of thought and behavior. Exposure therapy reduces phobias and post-traumatic stress, and dialectical behavior therapy lowers self-harm in people with intense emotional instability. Behavioral techniques support children with developmental difficulties, and medication combined with therapy helps many people with severe mental illness live independently.\n\nThe benefits reach well beyond the clinic. Psychological research informs how children are taught, how workplaces are organized, how public health campaigns encourage vaccination or quitting smoking, and how products are designed to reduce human error.\n\nScientific testing also protects people by revealing which interventions are not beneficial or may even be harmful. Programs that tried to frighten teenagers away from crime, for instance, were found to increase offending, and some forms of immediate debriefing after trauma appear to hinder recovery. Good intentions are not enough. Because psychology subjects its therapies to evidence, it can offer help that is effective, honest about its limits, and capable of improving as knowledge grows."}
{"id": "cur-essay-200", "group": "gb-essay-200.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Prompt:\n\nDiscuss the importance of a digital engagement policy for an organization's online presence and its impact on employees, brand reputation, and overall success. Provide specific examples of how a well-implemented policy can contribute to increased commitment, improved relationships with stakeholders, and effective marketing strategies. Additionally, discuss the potential challenges and considerations in implementing and maintaining such a policy, including how to effectively handle negative feedback and adapt marketing approaches.", "text": "An organization's online presence is no longer managed only by its marketing department. Every employee with a social media account, every customer review, and every reply to a complaint contributes to how the organization is perceived. A digital engagement policy, which sets out how the organization and its people communicate online, is therefore essential to protecting reputation and supporting long-term success.\n\nFor employees, a clear policy removes uncertainty. It explains who may speak on behalf of the company, what information is confidential, how to disclose an affiliation when discussing products, and what conduct is expected in personal posts that could be linked to the employer. When guidance is combined with training and trust, staff become confident advocates. Companies that encourage employees to share their work and achievements often find that these posts reach wider audiences and are considered more credible than official advertising, and employees who feel trusted tend to be more committed.\n\nA well-implemented policy also strengthens relationships with stakeholders. Setting standards for tone and response times means customers receive consistent, respectful answers. An airline that replies to delayed passengers quickly and helpfully on social media, for example, can turn frustration into loyalty. Consistent messaging across platforms supports marketing strategy, and guidelines on data protection and advertising rules reduce legal risk. Investors, regulators, and community partners gain confidence from communication that is transparent and coordinated.\n\nImplementation presents challenges. A policy that is too restrictive can discourage genuine engagement and may conflict with employees' legal rights to discuss working conditions, while one that is too vague provides no protection. Platforms and norms change rapidly, so the document needs regular review, and it is effective only if people actually read it and managers model it.\n\nNegative feedback deserves particular attention. The policy should direct staff to respond promptly, acknowledge the problem, avoid arguing in public, and move detailed discussion to private channels. Deleting legitimate criticism usually makes matters worse. An escalation plan should define when a complaint becomes a crisis and who takes charge. Monitoring feedback and analytics also allows the organization to adapt its marketing, dropping approaches that alienate audiences and building on those that resonate."}
{"id": "cur-essay-736", "group": "gb-essay-736.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Explain the importance of differentiation and integration in organizational structure and discuss the advantages and challenges associated with each approach. Provide examples of companies that successfully implement these strategies and explain how their choices of coordination methods contribute to their overall effectiveness.", "text": "Every organization faces two opposing demands. It must divide work among specialized units so that complex tasks are done well, and it must bring those units back together so that they pursue common goals. Paul Lawrence and Jay Lorsch called these processes differentiation and integration, and their research in the 1960s found that successful firms achieve high levels of both, matched to the uncertainty of their environment.\n\nDifferentiation refers to the division of an organization into departments with distinct tasks, skills, time horizons, and attitudes. Its advantages are expertise and responsiveness. Researchers can concentrate on long-term discovery, salespeople on customers, and production staff on efficiency, and separate divisions can adapt to different products or regions. The cost is that specialized groups develop their own priorities and language. Conflict, duplication, and narrow thinking increase as units grow apart.\n\nIntegration is the coordination of these units. Simple methods include hierarchy, rules, and plans, while richer methods include liaison roles, cross-functional teams, dedicated integrating managers, and matrix structures. Integration produces consistency, shared resources, and faster joint decisions, but it consumes managerial time, can slow action through meetings and dual reporting, and may suppress local initiative if overused.\n\nCompanies resolve the tension in different ways. Johnson & Johnson has long operated as a highly differentiated family of autonomous businesses, which keeps each close to its market, and it relies on a shared credo, common financial controls, and corporate leadership to hold the whole together. Procter & Gamble uses a matrix that combines global product units with regional selling organizations, so that brand strategy is unified while execution fits local markets. Apple is organized by function, with deep specialists in hardware, software, and design, and it integrates them through senior leaders who are experts themselves and through intense collaboration on each product. Toyota coordinates engineering, production, and suppliers through powerful chief engineers and cross-functional teams.\n\nIn each case, effectiveness comes from fit. The more differentiated the organization and the more interdependent its units, the stronger the coordination mechanisms it requires. Firms that specialize without integrating fragment, and those that integrate without real specialization lose the expertise that made them competitive."}
{"id": "cur-essay-51", "group": "gb-essay-51.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "\"How does the concept of sustainability impact the fashion industry and social inequality within it?\"", "text": "Sustainability has become one of the most discussed ideas in fashion, largely because the industry's current model is so clearly unsustainable. Clothing production consumes vast quantities of water, relies on synthetic fibres derived from fossil fuels, pollutes rivers with dyes, and generates mountains of waste as garments are worn a few times and discarded. Fast fashion has accelerated all of this by making new styles available every week at very low prices. In response, brands now promote organic cotton, recycled polyester, resale platforms, and repair services, and regulators, especially in Europe, are beginning to require more durable products and more honest environmental claims.\n\nSustainability, however, is not only an environmental question. Low prices depend on low wages, and most garment workers are women in countries such as Bangladesh, Cambodia, and India who earn far less than a living wage. The collapse of the Rana Plaza factory complex in 2013, which killed more than 1,100 people, exposed the human cost of pressure from buyers for speed and cheapness. A shirt made from recycled fibre by an underpaid worker in an unsafe building is not sustainable in any meaningful sense. Genuine reform has to include fair pay, safe conditions, and the right to organize.\n\nThe movement also creates inequalities of its own. Ethically made clothing usually costs more, so sustainable fashion can become a privilege of wealthier consumers, while people on low incomes are criticized for buying the only clothes they can afford. Some brands exploit the trend through greenwashing, advertising a small eco-friendly collection while their overall production keeps growing. Meanwhile, much of the used clothing donated in rich countries is shipped to places such as Ghana and Chile, where it overwhelms local markets and landfills.\n\nSustainability therefore has the potential either to reduce inequality in fashion or to deepen it. The outcome depends on whether responsibility is placed on companies and governments, through supply chain transparency, binding safety agreements, and wage commitments, or left to individual shoppers. A fashion industry that is truly sustainable would produce less, pay more, and share its benefits more evenly."}
{"id": "cur-essay-923", "group": "gb-essay-923.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "\"In light of the high rates of recidivism in the US correctional system, discuss Brandon Matthews' proposed alternative approach to punishment and rehabilitation. What are the key elements of his model and how does it aim to address the shortcomings of the current system? Explore the potential challenges and criticisms that may arise in implementing this reform. In your opinion, is Matthews' model a valid solution to the problems within the prison system? Provide evidence to support your stance.\"", "text": "The American correctional system releases hundreds of thousands of people each year, and most return. Federal studies tracking state prisoners have found that roughly two-thirds are rearrested within three years and more than four-fifths within nine. Those figures suggest that prison, as currently run, neither deters reoffending nor prepares people for lawful life. Brandon Matthews argues that this failure is built into the system's design and that a different model is required.\n\nAs I understand his argument, the core problem is that American corrections has swung between two goals, punishment and rehabilitation, without succeeding at either. Punitive policies isolate people, sever family ties, and return them to society with fewer skills and more stigma. Rehabilitation programs, meanwhile, are often delivered inside the same punitive environment and treat the person as a passive recipient of services. Matthews proposes moving beyond this pendulum. The key elements of his approach are that the incarcerated person takes an active part in his or her own change, that accountability means repairing harm instead of simply enduring time, that education, work, and treatment are central to a sentence and not privileges, and that preparation for release, including community support, begins on the first day.\n\nImplementation would face real obstacles. Many voters and victims equate justice with severity, and politicians fear appearing lenient. Programs cost money up front, prisons are overcrowded and understaffed, and staff culture trained in control does not change quickly. Critics also question whether such a model can work for people who have committed serious violence.\n\nIn my view the model is a valid direction, though not a complete solution. The evidence supports its central claims. A major RAND analysis found that prisoners who took part in education programs had substantially lower odds of returning to prison, and countries such as Norway, which organize prisons around normal life and reintegration, report much lower reoffending. Still, reform inside prisons cannot succeed alone. Without housing, employment, and treatment after release, and without reducing the number of people imprisoned in the first place, even the best-designed model will be asked to do too much."}
{"id": "cur-essay-83", "group": "gb-essay-83.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the historical context and implications of the Canadian government's legislation, such as Bill C-31, and its impact on Indigenous rights and identity. How has the recognition of past injustices and the understanding of history motivated individuals to advocate for the restoration of Indigenous rights and interests?", "text": "For more than a century, the Canadian government used the Indian Act of 1876 to decide who counted as an \"Indian\" in law. The definition was not neutral. A First Nations woman who married a man without status lost her own status, as did her children, while a non-Indigenous woman who married a status man gained it. Women who lost status could be forced to leave their reserves and were denied band membership, treaty benefits, and often burial among their relatives. The rule served a broader policy of assimilation, steadily reducing the number of people to whom the Crown owed obligations.\n\nIndigenous women led the resistance. Jeannette Corbiere Lavell and Yvonne Bédard challenged the provision in the 1970s and lost narrowly at the Supreme Court. Sandra Lovelace then took her case to the United Nations Human Rights Committee, which found Canada in violation of international law in 1981. With the equality guarantee of the Charter of Rights and Freedoms taking effect in 1985, Parliament passed Bill C-31. It ended the loss of status through marriage, restored status to those who had been stripped of it, and gave bands more control over their own membership.\n\nThe results were mixed. More than one hundred thousand people regained status, an important act of recognition. Yet the bill created new categories of registration and a \"second-generation cut-off\" under which status ends after two successive generations of parenting with non-status partners. Descendants of reinstated women were still treated less favourably than descendants of men, and many bands received new members without additional land or funding, which caused tension within communities.\n\nThese flaws motivated further advocacy. Sharon McIvor and later plaintiffs won court rulings that led to amendments in 2011 and 2017. Their efforts were strengthened by a wider public reckoning with history, including the Truth and Reconciliation Commission's findings on residential schools and Canada's commitment to the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples.\n\nUnderstanding this past has changed the argument. Advocates no longer ask only for fairer rules within the Indian Act. They assert that Indigenous nations themselves, not the federal government, should determine who belongs to them."}
{"id": "cur-essay-426", "group": "gb-essay-426.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Compare and contrast the strategies used by clothing firms in monopolistically competitive industries and steel manufacturing companies in oligopolistic competitive industries to differentiate themselves from their competitors. Analyze how factors such as product quality, pricing, advertising, and market entry barriers contribute to their competitive advantage in their respective industries.", "text": "Clothing companies and steel producers both seek to stand apart from rivals, but the structure of their industries leads them to do so in very different ways. Clothing is a typical example of monopolistic competition, with many sellers, easy entry, and products that are similar but not identical. Steel manufacturing is an oligopoly, in which a small number of large firms supply a largely standardized product and each must consider how the others will react.\n\nIn clothing, differentiation is the foundation of competition. Because a T-shirt from one firm can substitute for another, companies try to make consumers perceive a difference through design, fit, fabric quality, and above all brand image. Nike sells performance and aspiration, Zara sells speed in following trends, and Patagonia sells durability and environmental values. Advertising, celebrity endorsement, and social media are major expenses because they build loyalty that allows a firm to charge more than its costs alone would justify. Pricing varies widely, from discount to luxury, and signals a brand's position. Barriers to entry are low, since production can be outsourced and products sold online, so any successful style is quickly imitated and firms must keep innovating.\n\nIn steel, the product is close to a commodity, so differentiation is harder and less visible. Producers compete on cost efficiency, consistent quality, reliable delivery, and technical service, and some specialize in advanced grades for automobiles, construction, or energy. Advertising to the public is minimal, and relationships with industrial buyers matter more. Pricing is interdependent. If one firm cuts prices, others are likely to follow, so firms often avoid price wars and watch the leaders. Nucor gained an advantage through lower-cost electric furnace mini-mills, not through branding.\n\nBarriers to entry explain much of the contrast. A steel mill requires billions in capital, economies of scale, access to raw materials, and often support from trade policy, which protects established producers. Clothing firms enjoy no such protection, so their advantage rests on intangible assets that must be renewed constantly.\n\nIn short, clothing firms compete mainly for the customer's imagination, while steel firms compete on cost, scale, and reliability, with entry barriers providing the security that brands provide in fashion."}
{"id": "cur-essay-384", "group": "gb-essay-384.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "prompt: Discuss the role of Korean popular culture, specifically K-pop music and Korean dramas, in promoting rural and urban places in South Korea. How does the book \"Korean Popular Culture and the Selling of Place\" by Youjeong Oh analyze the strategies used by South Korea to attract tourists through the use of K-pop, K-drama, K-food, plastic surgery, and tourism? How does the book examine the relationship between urban planning and Hallyu? In what ways does Korean pop culture shape the commodification of rural and urban regions in South Korea?", "text": "Youjeong Oh's study of Korean popular culture and the selling of place argues that the Korean Wave, or Hallyu, is not only an export industry but also a tool of urban and regional development. Her central claim is that television dramas and K-pop have become means through which South Korean cities and counties market themselves, and that the alliance between local governments and entertainment producers has changed how places are imagined, built, and consumed.\n\nThe first half of her analysis concerns television drama and smaller or rural localities. After local elections were introduced in the 1990s, mayors and governors came under pressure to show visible achievements and to compete for visitors and investment. Many chose to sponsor drama productions, offering funds, sets, and filming sites in exchange for exposure on screen. When a series succeeded, fans from Korea and from Japan, China, and Southeast Asia travelled to its locations, and towns promoted themselves through the romance of the story. Oh stresses how speculative this strategy is. Popularity is unpredictable and brief, expensive sets are often left abandoned, and public money carries the risk while producers gain the subsidy.\n\nThe second half turns to K-pop and Seoul. Idols are tied to consumption districts instead of scenery. Gangnam, home to entertainment agencies, has promoted itself through idol imagery and themed streets, attracting fans who hope to be near the stars, and it also markets cosmetic surgery clinics to visitors drawn by the look that idols embody. Myeong-dong uses the faces of celebrities to sell cosmetics, fashion, and food to tourists. Here pop culture supports a wider tourism economy that includes shopping, K-food, and beauty services.\n\nOn the relationship between urban planning and Hallyu, Oh shows that planning becomes a matter of image-making. Instead of investing in lasting infrastructure or residents' needs, authorities borrow the emotional power of media content, and fans' attachment does the work of promotion.\n\nThe result is the commodification of place. Rural and urban areas alike are packaged as backdrops to be consumed, their identities defined by outside audiences and temporary trends. Oh's account is valuable because it reveals both the ingenuity of this strategy and its fragility."}
{"id": "cur-essay-390", "group": "gb-essay-390.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "How does leadership play a critical role in the criminal justice system? How do leaders guide change and instill high standards in law enforcement? Discuss the importance of managers in the criminal justice system and their role in optimizing processes and achieving reliable outcomes.", "text": "The criminal justice system holds extraordinary power over people's lives. Police officers may stop, search, and arrest, prosecutors decide whom to charge, and corrections staff control the daily existence of those in custody. Because so much depends on individual discretion, the quality of leadership within these institutions largely determines whether that power is used fairly and effectively.\n\nLeaders play a critical role first by defining purpose and culture. A police chief, a district attorney, or a prison warden communicates through words and example what the organization values. If leaders reward only arrest numbers, officers will pursue arrests. If they emphasize problem solving, de-escalation, and respect for rights, those priorities shape behavior on the street. Subordinates watch closely how leaders respond to misconduct, and tolerance of small violations quickly teaches that standards are negotiable.\n\nLeaders also guide change. Criminal justice agencies are traditional, hierarchical, and often resistant to reform, yet they face new technology, changing laws, and public demands for accountability. Effective leaders explain why change is necessary, involve staff and community members in planning, provide training, and remain consistent when resistance appears. The adoption of community policing, body-worn cameras, and crisis intervention teams succeeded where leaders treated them as changes in practice and not merely as new equipment.\n\nHigh standards are instilled through concrete systems. Careful recruitment, thorough field training, clear policies on the use of force, early warning systems that identify troubling patterns, and fair, transparent discipline all translate values into expectations. Leaders who are honest about failures and who support officers' wellbeing earn the credibility needed to demand integrity.\n\nManagers complement this work by making the system function day to day. They allocate personnel and budgets, supervise casework, and use data to find delays, errors, and disparities. A court administrator who reduces case backlogs, a laboratory manager who enforces evidence procedures, or a probation supervisor who monitors caseloads helps ensure that outcomes are consistent instead of dependent on chance. Reliable processes protect both public safety and the rights of the accused.\n\nLeadership supplies direction and legitimacy, and management supplies order and reliability. A justice system needs both to deserve the public's trust."}
{"id": "cur-essay-796", "group": "gb-essay-796.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "To what extent does aggression play a significant role in human evolution, based on the manifestations of aggression among chimpanzees and bonobos and their causes and comparisons?", "text": "Chimpanzees and bonobos are our closest living relatives, and their behavior is often used to ask whether aggression is a deep part of human nature. The comparison suggests that aggression has played a significant role in human evolution, but not a simple or exclusive one.\n\nChimpanzees provide the strongest case for aggression as an evolved strategy. Males form coalitions, patrol the borders of their territory, and sometimes attack and kill isolated individuals from neighboring groups. Long-term observations at sites such as Gombe and Ngogo show that communities that eliminate rivals can expand their range, gaining food and reproductive opportunities. Within groups, males fight for rank, coerce females, and occasionally commit infanticide. A large comparative study found that such killings occur across many communities regardless of human disturbance, which indicates that they are adaptive behavior, not an artifact of habitat loss.\n\nBonobos appear very different. Females form alliances that restrain male dominance, encounters between groups can be tolerant and even friendly, and lethal attacks have not been reliably documented. A common explanation is ecological. Bonobos live south of the Congo River, where food is more abundant and gorillas are absent, so females can travel together and support one another. Some researchers argue that selection against aggressive males made bonobos effectively self-domesticated. Recent work complicates the peaceful image, since male bonobos squabble frequently, but their aggression rarely escalates to coalition violence.\n\nHumans show features of both species. Evidence of ancient massacres and the universality of warfare suggest that coalitionary aggression between groups, similar to that of chimpanzees, has shaped our history. Yet within groups, people are remarkably tolerant compared with any ape, and human success depends on cooperation, sharing, and teaching. Richard Wrangham has proposed that humans evolved low reactive aggression, perhaps because groups punished bullies, while retaining a capacity for planned, collective violence.\n\nAggression has therefore been an important selective force, particularly in competition between groups, but its expression depends heavily on ecology and social structure. The contrast between two closely related apes shows that violence is a flexible potential, and that cooperation has been at least as central to making us human."}
{"id": "cur-essay-354", "group": "gb-essay-354.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Discuss the causes and implications of infertility, exploring both the genetic and environmental factors that can contribute to this issue. Additionally, examine the gender disparities in infertility rates and the potential psychological effects on individuals facing this challenge. Finally, consider the limitations and potential solutions for addressing infertility in the United States.", "text": "Infertility is usually defined as the inability to conceive after twelve months of regular unprotected intercourse, and it affects a substantial share of couples in the United States and worldwide. Its causes are varied, and in many cases more than one factor is involved.\n\nGenetic factors contribute in both sexes. In men, Klinefelter syndrome and small deletions on the Y chromosome impair sperm production, and mutations in the cystic fibrosis gene can cause absence of the ducts that carry sperm. In women, Turner syndrome and certain variants of the fragile X gene lead to early loss of ovarian function. Conditions such as polycystic ovary syndrome and endometriosis also run in families. Environmental and lifestyle influences are at least as important. Fertility declines with age, especially for women after the mid-thirties. Smoking, heavy alcohol use, obesity, and very low body weight disrupt hormones and gamete quality. Untreated sexually transmitted infections can scar the fallopian tubes, and exposure to pesticides, industrial chemicals, radiation, and heat can reduce sperm counts.\n\nAlthough infertility is often regarded as a woman's problem, male factors are the sole cause in roughly a third of cases and a contributing cause in many others. Women nonetheless bear most of the burden. They undergo the majority of testing and invasive treatment, even when the difficulty lies with their partner, and in many communities they receive most of the blame. Men, meanwhile, are frequently evaluated late or not at all and may feel unable to discuss the issue.\n\nThe psychological effects can be severe. People describe grief, shame, anxiety, and depression, along with strain on marriages, sexual relationships, and friendships. The monthly cycle of hope and disappointment and the stress of treatment intensify these feelings.\n\nIn the United States, the main limitation is access. A single cycle of in vitro fertilization commonly costs well over ten thousand dollars, insurance coverage varies widely by state and employer, and clinics are concentrated in wealthy urban areas, which leaves lower-income and minority patients underserved. Possible solutions include broader insurance mandates, routine early evaluation of both partners, better education about age and fertility, prevention and treatment of infections, and integrating counseling into fertility care."}
{"id": "cur-essay-486", "group": "gb-essay-486.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Describe a cultural dance performance that you have witnessed or heard about. Discuss the movements, objects used, and the cultural significance of the dance. Reflect on your personal impressions and the impact it had on you.", "text": "The dance I want to describe is one I have never witnessed in person. What I know of hula kahiko, the ancient style of Hawaiian hula, comes from descriptions of performances at the Merrie Monarch Festival, held each spring in Hilo on the island of Hawaii, and those accounts have left a strong impression on me.\n\nHula kahiko is performed to chant, not to melody. A chanter kneels at the side of the stage and strikes a gourd drum called the ipu heke, or a sharkskin-covered drum called the pahu, while delivering poetry in the Hawaiian language. The dancers, often a large group moving in precise unison, keep their knees bent and their feet close to the ground. Their hips sway steadily while their arms and hands tell the story, tracing the rise of a mountain, the fall of rain, the movement of waves, or the flight of a bird. Faces are expressive and eyes follow the hands. The style is powerful and grounded, quite unlike the gentle image of hula sold to tourists.\n\nThe objects used carry meaning. Dancers may shake feathered gourd rattles, strike split bamboo sticks against their bodies, or click smooth stones together in their palms. They wear skirts of ti leaf or patterned cloth and garlands of ferns and flowers on the head, wrists, and ankles. Many of these plants are gathered from the forest with prayers, because they are associated with Laka, the goddess of hula.\n\nThe cultural significance is considerable. Before Hawaiian was written, hula and chant preserved genealogies, history, and stories of gods such as Pele. Missionaries condemned the dance in the nineteenth century, and it was pushed out of public life until King Kalakaua encouraged its revival. A broader renaissance in the 1970s made hula a symbol of Hawaiian identity and language. Students train for years under a teacher within a school that functions almost as a family.\n\nWhat strikes me most is the idea that a dance can be a library. Learning about hula changed how I think about memory, showing me that knowledge can be stored in bodies and voices as faithfully as on a page."}
{"id": "cur-essay-849", "group": "gb-essay-849.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "essay/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Analyze the factors that contribute to poor maternal health outcomes among black women and propose systemic changes that can be implemented globally to improve black maternal health. In your response, consider the role of societal attitudes, healthcare access, and intersectionality in shaping the experiences of black women during pregnancy and childbirth.", "text": "Black women die from pregnancy-related causes at far higher rates than other women in countries where data are collected by race. In the United States, the maternal mortality rate for Black women is roughly three times that of white women, and in the United Kingdom a similar gap has been reported for years. Most of these deaths are considered preventable. The disparity cannot be explained by biology, and it persists among Black women with high incomes and advanced education, which points to causes within society and the health system.\n\nSocietal attitudes are a major factor. Studies and personal testimony show that Black women's reports of pain and warning symptoms are more likely to be dismissed or treated late, reflecting stereotypes and implicit bias among clinicians. Beyond individual encounters, a lifetime of exposure to racism produces chronic stress that raises the risk of hypertension, preeclampsia, and other complications, a process researchers call weathering.\n\nAccess to care compounds the problem. Black women are more likely to be uninsured or underinsured, to live in areas where maternity units have closed, and to give birth in hospitals with fewer resources and poorer safety records. Across sub-Saharan Africa and the Caribbean, shortages of skilled birth attendants, blood supplies, and emergency obstetric services account for a large share of global maternal deaths.\n\nIntersectionality helps explain why these burdens accumulate. A Black woman's experience is shaped at once by race, gender, class, immigration status, and geography. A poor, rural, or migrant mother faces barriers that no single category captures, and policies aimed at only one dimension tend to miss her.\n\nSeveral systemic changes could be applied worldwide. Governments should guarantee affordable coverage throughout pregnancy and for a full year after birth. Hospitals should adopt standardized protocols for hemorrhage and hypertension so that treatment does not depend on a clinician's judgment of who deserves urgency. Midwives, doulas, and community health workers from the communities they serve should be trained and funded. Every maternal death should be reviewed, with data published by race, and institutions held accountable for gaps. Investment in housing, nutrition, and primary care must address the conditions that make pregnancy dangerous before it begins."}
{"id": "cur-news-WilliamKazer-20", "group": "gb-reuter-WilliamKazer/20.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Heavy losses in metals trading by a young employee at big Chinese conglomerate CITIC showed striking similarities to Nick Leeson's deals that humbled Barings Bank, a top CITIC official said.", "text": "A senior official at China International Trust and Investment Corp (CITIC) has compared heavy metals trading losses run up by one of the conglomerate's young employees to the unauthorised dealing by Nick Leeson that brought down Britain's Barings Bank last year.\n\nThe official, who asked not to be named, said the trader, a man in his late twenties working for a CITIC trading subsidiary in Shanghai, had built up large positions in copper futures on the London Metal Exchange without the knowledge of his superiors and had hidden the mounting losses for months.\n\n\"The parallels are uncomfortable,\" the official said. \"A young man who was trusted, who was seen as a star, who was allowed both to trade and to settle his own business. When the market moved against him, he doubled his bets instead of admitting the problem.\"\n\nHe put the losses at around $40 million, a figure that is small against CITIC's total assets but large enough to embarrass a company long regarded as the most internationally sophisticated of China's state-owned enterprises. CITIC, founded in 1979 as Beijing's investment window on the world, has interests ranging from banking and aviation to property and power generation.\n\nThe official said the trader had been detained and that several managers had been removed from their posts pending an internal investigation. The company has since separated its dealing and back office functions and imposed strict limits on overseas futures positions, he added.\n\nTraders in Hong Kong and London said the affair underlined the risks Chinese firms took as they rushed into international derivatives markets in the early 1990s, often with little experience and weak internal controls. Beijing's securities regulator barred most state firms from trading futures abroad in 1994, but enforcement has been patchy.\n\n\"There were a lot of Chinese names in the market who simply did not understand what they were doing,\" said one metals broker in London. \"Some of them were punting with the state's money and nobody at home was checking the tickets.\"\n\nThe CITIC official said the company would absorb the losses in full and that its credit standing would not be affected."}
{"id": "cur-news-JonathanBirt-12", "group": "gb-reuter-JonathanBirt/12.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "British health and industrial gases giant BOC Group Plc reported its best-ever set of full-year profits on Tuesday, dispelling some of the gloom which has settled over its share price in recent months.", "text": "BOC Group Plc, the British industrial gases and health care company, posted record annual profits on Tuesday, offering some relief to investors who have watched its shares lag the wider market for much of the year.\n\nPretax profit for the year to September 30 rose 11 percent to 445 million pounds ($730 million), ahead of analysts' forecasts, which had ranged from 425 million to 440 million pounds. Turnover climbed eight percent to 4.02 billion pounds, and the company lifted its total dividend to 27.3 pence a share from 25.4 pence.\n\nThe shares, which had slipped about 12 percent since the spring on worries about the health care division and the strength of sterling, rose 21 pence to 903 pence in early London trading.\n\n\"These are the best results in the group's history, and they reflect strong demand for gases in almost every market where we operate,\" the company's chief executive told reporters. \"We think the concerns of recent months have been overdone.\"\n\nThe gases business, which accounts for roughly three quarters of group earnings, was again the main driver. Operating profit there rose 14 percent, helped by new plants in China, Thailand and South Korea and by firm demand from electronics and steel customers. BOC said it planned to invest more than 600 million pounds in new gases capacity over the coming year, much of it in Asia.\n\nHealth care was weaker. Profits in the division fell six percent as hospitals in the United States continued to squeeze prices for anaesthetics and medical equipment, although the company said sales of its newer anaesthetic agents were growing. The vacuum technology unit, which supplies pumps to semiconductor makers, reported a sharp rise in orders.\n\nAnalysts said the figures should ease, though not remove, doubts about the group's direction. \"The gases numbers are excellent and the dividend is a statement of confidence,\" said one chemicals analyst at a London brokerage. \"The question that has not gone away is what they do with health care.\"\n\nBOC said trading in the first weeks of the new financial year had been in line with its expectations."}
{"id": "cur-news-MartinWolk-7", "group": "gb-reuter-MartinWolk/7.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Boeing Co.", "text": "Boeing Co. said on Thursday it would raise production of its commercial jets for the second time this year and hire several thousand more workers, as a surge in airline orders stretched the capacity of its factories around Seattle.\n\nThe world's largest aircraft maker said combined output of its five jetliner models would rise to 40 planes a month by the end of 1997, up from about 22 a month at present and above the target of 36 it announced in the spring. Production of the 737, its best-selling aircraft, will increase to 21 a month from 10, while output of the 747 jumbo jet will climb to five a month.\n\nTo meet the higher rates, Boeing plans to add about 5,000 employees in the Puget Sound region by the middle of next year, on top of the 8,200 it has already taken on since January. The company cut nearly 30,000 jobs between 1990 and 1995 as airlines, battered by recession and heavy losses, cancelled or delayed deliveries.\n\n\"Our customers are profitable again, traffic is growing and they need aircraft sooner than we had planned to build them,\" said the head of the company's commercial airplane group. \"Our challenge is to increase production without sacrificing quality or our delivery commitments.\"\n\nBoeing has booked orders for more than 500 jetliners so far this year, its best performance since 1990, including large purchases by carriers in Asia and by U.S. airlines replacing older fleets. Its order backlog stands at roughly $80 billion.\n\nAnalysts welcomed the announcement but warned that the rapid increase carried risks. Suppliers of engines, forgings and interior fittings were already struggling to keep pace, they said, and thousands of newly hired workers would need months of training.\n\n\"Going from 22 to 40 a month in little more than a year is an enormous undertaking,\" said an aerospace analyst at a Seattle investment firm. \"They have done it before, but not without some pain along the way.\"\n\nBoeing shares rose $1.25 to $94.50 on the New York Stock Exchange. The company said it did not expect the higher rates to change its financial outlook for the current year."}
{"id": "cur-news-AlexanderSmith-1", "group": "gb-reuter-AlexanderSmith/1.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Top managers in major financial firms need to be razor-sharp if the world's increasingly complex markets are to run smoothly, however sophisticated the regulatory regime, regulators and politicians said on Thursday.", "text": "No system of financial regulation, however elaborate, can substitute for alert and well-informed senior management at the banks and securities houses that dominate global markets, regulators and politicians told a conference in London on Thursday.\n\nSpeaker after speaker at the gathering of supervisors, bankers and legislators returned to the same theme: the trading scandals of recent years, from the collapse of Barings to the losses at Daiwa Bank and Sumitomo Corp, were failures of management before they were failures of regulation.\n\n\"We can write rules until the shelves collapse under the weight of them,\" the chairman of Britain's Securities and Investments Board told delegates. \"But if the people at the top of a firm do not understand the business being done in their name, and do not ask awkward questions when profits look too good to be true, the rules will not save them.\"\n\nHe said supervisors increasingly wanted to judge the quality of a firm's senior managers and its internal controls, and not simply to check its capital ratios. Directors who could not explain how their derivatives desks made money should not be running them, he added.\n\nA senior U.S. banking regulator agreed, saying the speed and complexity of modern markets meant problems could grow from manageable to fatal within days. \"The first line of defence is in the firm itself. Regulators are the second line at best, and often we arrive after the event,\" he said.\n\nPoliticians at the conference said public confidence depended on individuals being held to account. A member of the British parliament's Treasury committee said it was striking how few senior executives had lost their jobs, let alone faced sanctions, after losses running to hundreds of millions of pounds.\n\nBankers present accepted much of the criticism but warned against overreaction. The chief executive of one European investment bank said firms had spent heavily on risk management systems since the Barings affair and that boards were far more engaged than they were two years ago.\n\n\"Nobody in this industry wants to be the next headline,\" he said. \"That fear has concentrated minds more effectively than any rulebook.\""}
{"id": "cur-news-EricAuchard-11", "group": "gb-reuter-EricAuchard/11.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "CompuServe Corp., acknowledging its failure to attract U.S.", "text": "CompuServe Corp. said on Thursday it was abandoning its effort to win over mainstream American households and would concentrate on the business and professional users who have long been its most loyal customers, conceding that its push into the mass consumer market had failed.\n\nThe online service, the second largest in the United States behind America Online Inc., said it would close its family-oriented WOW! service at the end of January, less than a year after its launch, and take a charge of about $35 million against earnings in the current quarter. WOW!, which was aimed at novice computer users, attracted only about 100,000 subscribers.\n\n\"We tried to be all things to all people, and that was a mistake,\" the company's chief executive said on a conference call with analysts. \"Our strength is with people who use online services for work and who are prepared to pay for quality information. That is where we will invest.\"\n\nCompuServe, which is 80 percent owned by tax preparation company H&R Block Inc., has seen its U.S. membership stagnate at around three million while America Online has more than doubled in size to about seven million, helped by aggressive marketing and a switch to flat-rate pricing. CompuServe's shares have lost more than half their value since the company went public in April.\n\nThe company said it would cut its marketing budget sharply, ending the mass mailing of free trial disks, and would spend more on its network services division, which sells data communications to corporations and has remained solidly profitable. It also plans to rebuild its main service around Internet standards.\n\nAnalysts said the retreat was an overdue recognition of reality. \"They were never going to outspend AOL, and they were losing money trying,\" said an analyst at a New York brokerage. \"The business customer base is smaller, but it is profitable and it is theirs.\"\n\nCompuServe said it expected to report a loss for the fiscal year but to return to profitability in the following year."}
{"id": "cur-news-KevinMorrison-19", "group": "gb-reuter-KevinMorrison/19.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Australian regional banks St George Bank Ltd and Advance Bank Australia Ltd on Monday unveiled a merger plan to create the nation's fifth largest bank with a market value of A$4.5 billion (US$3.55 billion).", "text": "St George Bank Ltd and Advance Bank Australia Ltd announced plans on Monday to merge, a deal that would create Australia's fifth largest bank, with a combined market value of about A$4.5 billion (US$3.55 billion), and a stronger challenger to the country's four dominant lenders.\n\nUnder the terms of the agreement, Sydney-based St George will offer cash and shares valuing Advance at A$2.65 billion, or about A$7.30 a share, a premium of roughly 18 percent to Advance's closing price on Friday. The boards of both banks have unanimously recommended the offer.\n\nThe merged group would have total assets of around A$45 billion, some 560 branches and more than four million customers, concentrated in New South Wales and South Australia, where Advance owns BankSA. It would rank behind National Australia Bank, Commonwealth Bank, Westpac and ANZ.\n\n\"This brings together two banks with very similar roots and very similar customers,\" St George's managing director told a news conference. \"Together we will have the scale to compete with the majors while remaining close to the communities we serve.\"\n\nBoth institutions began life as building societies and converted to banks only in the past decade. Analysts have long seen them as natural partners, and as likely takeover targets for the big four, although the federal government's stance against mergers among the majors has also cast doubt on whether they would be allowed to swallow strong regional competitors.\n\nThe banks said they expected annual cost savings of A$130 million within three years, mainly from combining head offices and computer systems. About 1,500 jobs are expected to go.\n\nAdvance shares jumped 92 cents to A$7.10, while St George slipped 14 cents to A$7.76 as investors weighed the price being paid.\n\n\"It is a full price, but strategically it makes a great deal of sense,\" said a banking analyst at a Sydney brokerage. \"The alternative for both of them was to be picked off one by one.\"\n\nThe deal requires the approval of Advance shareholders, the Reserve Bank of Australia and the federal treasurer, as well as clearance from the competition regulator."}
{"id": "cur-news-JoWinterbottom-20", "group": "gb-reuter-JoWinterbottom/20.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "British electricity transmission business National Grid should report a rise in half year profits on November 26 and could explain how it will cope with new pricing controls that come into effect on April 1, 1997.", "text": "National Grid, which owns and operates the high-voltage electricity transmission network in England and Wales, is expected to report higher half-year profits on November 26, when investors will also be looking for guidance on how it plans to live with a tougher price regime from next April.\n\nAnalysts forecast pretax profit for the six months to September 30 of between 275 million and 290 million pounds, up from 262 million a year earlier, with an interim dividend of around 4.3 pence a share. Cost cutting and lower interest charges are expected to account for most of the improvement.\n\nBut attention is likely to centre on the future. Industry regulator Offer has proposed that the company's transmission charges be cut by 20 percent in real terms on April 1, 1997, followed by further annual reductions of four percentage points below the rate of inflation for the following three years.\n\nNational Grid accepted the proposals last month, avoiding a referral to the Monopolies and Mergers Commission, but has so far said little about how it will protect earnings.\n\n\"The first half numbers are almost a sideshow,\" said a utilities analyst at one London broker. \"What the market wants to know is how much more they can take out of the cost base, and what that means for the dividend.\"\n\nThe company, which was floated on the stock market last December after being spun off from the 12 regional electricity companies, has already cut its workforce by about a quarter since privatisation of the industry in 1990. Analysts believe several hundred more jobs could go and that capital spending may be trimmed.\n\nInvestors will also be watching for news on the group's efforts to diversify. Its telecommunications arm Energis, which runs fibre optic cables along the grid's pylons, is still losing money, and analysts expect a first-half loss there of about 50 million pounds. A possible flotation or partial sale of Energis has been widely discussed.\n\nNational Grid shares have underperformed the wider market by around 10 percent since the regulator's proposals were published."}
{"id": "cur-news-PeterHumphrey-7", "group": "gb-reuter-PeterHumphrey/7.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "The Hong Kong government was in a flap on Thursday after a bombshell admission that Britain could not protect Hong Kong holders of British passports after China takes over the colony next July.", "text": "Hong Kong officials scrambled on Thursday to contain the fallout from an admission that Britain will be unable to offer consular protection to most of the territory's British passport holders once China resumes sovereignty next July.\n\nThe disclosure came late on Wednesday, when a senior official from the Security Branch told a Legislative Council panel that ethnic Chinese residents holding British National (Overseas) passports would be regarded by Beijing as Chinese citizens after the handover. As a result, he said, British diplomats would have no right to intervene on their behalf if they ran into trouble in Hong Kong or on the mainland.\n\n\"China does not recognise dual nationality. For these people the passport will be a travel document and nothing more while they are on Chinese soil,\" the official said in answer to a legislator's question.\n\nThe remark confirmed what lawyers had long suspected but what the government had never stated so bluntly, and it set off an uproar. Some 3.3 million of Hong Kong's 6.3 million people are eligible for the BN(O) passport, which carries no right to live in Britain.\n\nBy Thursday morning radio phone-in programmes were flooded with angry callers, and the government hurriedly called a news conference at which officials insisted that nothing had changed and that the passport would still guarantee easy travel to more than 80 countries.\n\n\"There is no new policy here and no cause for alarm,\" a government spokesman said. \"Britain will continue to offer full consular protection to BN(O) holders in third countries.\"\n\nLegislators were not reassured. \"People have been told for years that this passport means something,\" said a member of the Democratic Party. \"Now we learn that at the moment it matters most, in a Chinese police station, it is worth no more than the paper it is printed on.\"\n\nGovernor Chris Patten, who has pressed London without success to grant right of abode to more Hong Kong residents, was said by aides to be dismayed by the timing of the remark. The British Foreign Office in London declined to comment."}
{"id": "cur-news-TanEeLyn-8", "group": "gb-reuter-TanEeLyn/8.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Taiwan's call for a \"Greater China\" joint-venture bank on Wednesday was greeted with caution in Hong Kong, where analysts and businessmen said more details were needed to convince the industry of its feasibility.", "text": "A proposal from Taiwan for a jointly owned \"Greater China\" bank linking the island with Hong Kong and the Chinese mainland drew a guarded response in the British colony on Wednesday, with bankers and analysts saying the idea was too vague to judge.\n\nTaiwan's finance minister floated the plan in Taipei, suggesting that banks from the three economies could each take a stake in a new institution, probably based in Hong Kong, that would finance trade and investment flowing across the Taiwan Strait. Taiwanese companies have poured an estimated $25 billion into the mainland, most of it routed through Hong Kong because Taipei bans direct links.\n\nIn Hong Kong, the reaction was polite but sceptical. \"It is an interesting concept, but at the moment that is all it is,\" said the head of research at a local brokerage. \"Who would own it, who would regulate it and who would be lender of last resort? None of that has been spelled out.\"\n\nBankers noted that the timing was delicate. Hong Kong reverts to Chinese rule in the middle of next year, and Beijing has given no sign that it would welcome a venture conceived in Taipei, with which it has had no formal ties since 1949.\n\n\"Without a clear green light from Beijing, no serious Hong Kong bank is going to put capital into this,\" said a senior executive at one of the territory's Chinese-owned lenders. \"The politics come first, and the politics are not there yet.\"\n\nOthers questioned whether such a bank was needed at all. Hong Kong already has more than 180 licensed banks, many of them active in trade finance for Taiwanese manufacturers in Guangdong and Fujian provinces.\n\nSome businessmen were more receptive. A representative of a Taiwanese business association in Hong Kong said smaller firms often struggled to obtain credit for mainland projects because banks on the island could not lend directly and Hong Kong banks did not know them.\n\n\"If it makes borrowing easier and cheaper, our members would support it,\" he said. \"But we would need to see the fine print.\""}
{"id": "cur-news-MichaelConnor-12", "group": "gb-reuter-MichaelConnor/12.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "International businessman Tony O'Reilly said on Friday acquisitions by his Independent Newspapers Plc will slow considerably next year after the takeover of New Zealand's leading newspaper and other deals.", "text": "Tony O'Reilly, the Irish businessman who has built Independent Newspapers Plc into a publishing group spanning four continents, said on Friday the company's rapid run of acquisitions would slow markedly next year as it digests a string of purchases, including that of New Zealand's biggest newspaper publisher.\n\n\"We have had a very busy two years, and 1997 will be a year of consolidation,\" O'Reilly, the group's chairman, told reporters after a presentation to investors in New York. \"I would not expect anything like the same level of activity. Our job now is to make what we own work harder.\"\n\nDublin-based Independent has spent more than $900 million since 1994 buying into newspapers in Britain, South Africa, Australia and New Zealand. Its most recent move was to take control of Wilson & Horton Ltd, publisher of the New Zealand Herald in Auckland, in a deal valuing that company at about NZ$1.3 billion.\n\nO'Reilly, who is also chairman and chief executive of U.S. food company H.J. Heinz Co., said the spending spree had left Independent with higher debt than it was comfortable carrying over the long term, and that reducing borrowings would be a priority.\n\n\"We are not uncomfortable, but we are conscious of it,\" he said. \"The cash flows from these businesses are strong, and that is what will bring gearing down.\"\n\nHe did not rule out smaller deals, saying the company would look at \"bolt-on\" opportunities in markets where it already operates, but said there were no large transactions under consideration.\n\nAnalysts said the comments would be welcomed by shareholders, some of whom have worried that the pace of expansion was outrunning management's ability to integrate the new titles. \"The market wanted to hear that there is a pause coming,\" said a media analyst at a Dublin stockbroker. \"The assets are good ones. The issue has been the balance sheet.\"\n\nO'Reilly said he remained confident about the prospects for newspapers, despite the rise of electronic media, arguing that well-run regional titles enjoyed loyal readers and dependable advertising."}
{"id": "cur-news-AlexanderSmith-4", "group": "gb-reuter-AlexanderSmith/4.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "British property group MEPC said on Thursday it was pleased with a rise in pretax profits for the year but admitted disappointment at a fall in its net asset value (NAV) to 450 pence per share from 457 pence.", "text": "MEPC Plc, Britain's second largest property company, said on Thursday it was satisfied with an increase in annual pretax profits but acknowledged that a decline in the value of its portfolio, which pulled net asset value down to 450 pence a share from 457 pence, was a disappointment.\n\nPretax profit for the year to September 30 rose to 121.4 million pounds ($200 million) from 112.8 million, slightly ahead of market expectations, as lower interest costs and new lettings outweighed the loss of income from properties sold during the year. The total dividend was raised to 20.5 pence a share from 20.0 pence.\n\nBut the fall in net asset value, the measure most closely watched by property investors, took the shine off the figures. Analysts had been expecting a broadly unchanged outcome, and MEPC shares fell nine pence to 409 pence.\n\n\"We are pleased with the profit performance, which reflects a great deal of hard work on the portfolio,\" the company's chief executive said in an interview. \"On the net asset value, I will not pretend otherwise. We are disappointed, and we expect to do better.\"\n\nHe blamed the decline on weaker valuations for some of the group's secondary office buildings in Britain and on its Australian shopping centres, where rents have come under pressure. Values at its London offices and at its U.S. properties rose.\n\nMEPC has been reshaping its holdings, selling about 350 million pounds of smaller properties over the past 18 months and reinvesting in larger shopping centres and business parks. The chief executive said the programme was about two-thirds complete and that the benefits would show more clearly in the coming year.\n\nAnalysts said the results compared poorly with those of rivals such as Land Securities and British Land, which have recently reported rising asset values as the commercial property market recovers.\n\n\"The market has turned, and most of the sector is showing growth. MEPC is going backwards, even if only slightly,\" said a property analyst at a London securities house. \"It tells you the portfolio still has too many of the wrong buildings.\""}
{"id": "cur-news-JohnMastrini-12", "group": "gb-reuter-JohnMastrini/12.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Czech President Vaclav Havel on Thursday said he was deeply disturbed by a Chinese court's 11-year prison sentence on dissident Wang Dan who was accused of plotting to overthrow the government.", "text": "Czech President Vaclav Havel, who spent years in communist prisons for his own dissident activities, said on Thursday he was deeply disturbed by the 11-year jail sentence handed down by a Chinese court to pro-democracy activist Wang Dan.\n\nWang, a student leader of the 1989 Tiananmen Square protests, was convicted in Beijing on Wednesday of conspiring to subvert the government after a trial that lasted only a few hours. Foreign reporters and diplomats were barred from the courtroom.\n\n\"I am deeply disturbed by this verdict. A young man is being sent to prison for more than a decade for nothing other than expressing his opinions freely,\" Havel said in a statement released by his office at Prague Castle.\n\n\"I know from my own experience what such a sentence means, and I know how much it matters to a prisoner that the world outside does not forget him,\" the statement said. \"I appeal to the Chinese authorities to reconsider.\"\n\nHavel, a playwright who was jailed several times in the 1970s and 1980s before leading the peaceful revolution that toppled communist rule in Czechoslovakia in 1989, has been one of Europe's most outspoken advocates of human rights. His stance has sometimes sat uneasily with the centre-right government's efforts to expand trade with China.\n\nA spokesman for the president said Havel had asked the foreign ministry to convey his concern to the Chinese embassy in Prague. The ministry said it shared the president's view that the sentence was disproportionate, but gave no details of any formal protest.\n\nThe Chinese embassy rejected the criticism. \"Wang Dan is a criminal who has violated Chinese law. His case is an internal affair of China, and no foreign country or individual has the right to interfere,\" an embassy official said by telephone.\n\nThe United States, the European Union and several human rights organisations have also condemned the sentence. Wang, 27, had already served nearly four years in prison for his role in the 1989 demonstrations, which were crushed by the army.\n\nCzech human rights groups said they planned a candlelight vigil outside the Chinese embassy on Friday evening."}
{"id": "cur-news-DavidLawder-10", "group": "gb-reuter-DavidLawder/10.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Chrysler Corp.", "text": "Chrysler Corp. on Tuesday reported the best third-quarter earnings in its history, as strong demand for its minivans, Jeep sport utility vehicles and Dodge Ram pickup trucks more than offset the costs of launching several new models.\n\nThe No. 3 U.S. automaker said net income rose to $680 million, or $1.84 a share, from $354 million, or 91 cents a share, in the same period a year earlier, when production was disrupted by the changeover to a redesigned minivan line. Revenue grew 19 percent to $14.4 billion.\n\nThe results exceeded Wall Street forecasts of about $1.70 a share, and Chrysler stock rose 87.5 cents to $34.25 in morning trading on the New York Stock Exchange.\n\n\"This was a very strong quarter in what is usually our weakest period of the year,\" the company's chief financial officer told reporters at its headquarters in Auburn Hills, Michigan. \"Our plants are running flat out and we still cannot build enough of some vehicles.\"\n\nLight trucks, a category that includes minivans, pickups and sport utility vehicles, accounted for about two-thirds of Chrysler's U.S. sales in the quarter. Those vehicles carry far higher profit margins than passenger cars, and analysts estimate the company earns more than $6,000 on each Jeep Grand Cherokee it sells.\n\nChrysler's share of the U.S. car and truck market climbed to 16.1 percent from 14.3 percent a year earlier, the company said. Spending on rebates and other incentives fell to an average of about $700 a vehicle.\n\nThe company ended the quarter with $7.6 billion in cash, a cushion that management has said it needs to ride out the next downturn in the industry without the near-fatal distress it suffered in the early 1980s and again at the start of this decade.\n\nAnalysts said the figures confirmed that Chrysler was the most profitable of Detroit's Big Three on a per-vehicle basis, but some cautioned that its dependence on trucks left it exposed. \"If gasoline prices jump or consumer tastes shift, they have less to fall back on than Ford or GM,\" said an auto analyst at a New York investment bank."}
{"id": "cur-news-BenjaminKangLim-6", "group": "gb-reuter-BenjaminKangLim/6.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "China called on Taiwan on Tuesday to show goodwill by lifting a decades-old ban on direct trade and transport links, saying attempts by the island nation to block such links would be futile.", "text": "China urged Taiwan on Tuesday to demonstrate goodwill by scrapping its decades-old ban on direct trade, transport and postal links across the Taiwan Strait, and warned that efforts by the island's authorities to obstruct such ties were doomed to fail.\n\n\"Direct links are the common aspiration of compatriots on both sides and an inevitable trend,\" a spokesman for the cabinet's Taiwan Affairs Office told a news briefing in Beijing. \"Any attempt by the Taiwan authorities to delay or block them goes against the will of the people and will be futile.\"\n\nTaiwan has barred direct shipping, flights and mail with the mainland since 1949, when Nationalist forces fled to the island after losing a civil war to the Communists. Goods and travellers must pass through a third place, usually Hong Kong, adding time and cost.\n\nThe spokesman said the restrictions were hurting Taiwan's own businessmen, who have invested billions of dollars in factories on the mainland. He noted that Beijing had published regulations in August to allow cross-strait shipping between designated ports, and said it was waiting for a constructive response from Taipei.\n\n\"We have shown our sincerity. We hope the Taiwan side will take practical steps and stop setting up obstacles,\" he said.\n\nRelations between the two sides sank to their lowest point in years in March, when China held missile tests and war games near the island ahead of Taiwan's first direct presidential election. Semi-official talks have been suspended since mid-1995, when Taiwan President Lee Teng-hui made a private visit to the United States that infuriated Beijing.\n\nTaiwan's government says direct links can come only when Beijing stops threatening the island with force and treats it as an equal. In Taipei, an official of the Mainland Affairs Council said the issue involved national security and could not be rushed.\n\n\"If the mainland side truly wants progress, it should return to the negotiating table without preconditions,\" the official said.\n\nBusiness leaders in Taiwan have grown increasingly impatient with the ban, particularly as Hong Kong's return to Chinese rule next year raises questions about how indirect trade will be handled."}
{"id": "cur-news-ScottHillis-20", "group": "gb-reuter-ScottHillis/20.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Leading Chinese activist Wang Dan will go on trial on Wednesday, charged with the capital crime of plotting to overthrow the government, a family member said.", "text": "Wang Dan, one of China's best-known pro-democracy activists, will stand trial in Beijing on Wednesday on a charge of conspiring to overthrow the government, an offence that can carry the death penalty, a member of his family said on Monday.\n\nThe relative said the family received formal notice of the hearing from the Beijing No. 1 Intermediate People's Court at the weekend, giving them only a few days to prepare a defence. Wang's mother, a retired museum researcher, intends to act as one of his two defenders.\n\n\"We were told the trial will start on Wednesday morning. We have had very little time, and we have been allowed to see only part of the evidence,\" the family member said by telephone. \"We believe he is innocent and we will say so in court.\"\n\nWang, 27, was a history student at Peking University when he emerged as a leader of the 1989 demonstrations in Tiananmen Square. After the army crushed the protests, he topped the government's list of most wanted student organisers and served nearly four years in prison.\n\nFollowing his release in 1993 he continued to write essays for overseas publications and to sign petitions calling for political reform. Police took him from his home in May 1995, and he was held without charge for 17 months before being formally arrested this month.\n\nAccording to the family, the indictment cites articles Wang published in newspapers in Hong Kong and Taiwan, his contacts with exiled dissidents in the United States and his acceptance of a correspondence course scholarship from an American university.\n\nLegal experts said the minimum sentence for the charge was 10 years in prison.\n\nIt was not clear whether the trial would be open. Chinese law requires most trials to be held in public, but in sensitive cases seats are usually filled in advance with selected spectators.\n\nHuman rights groups abroad condemned the proceedings. \"The outcome has plainly been decided already,\" said a spokesman for a New York-based monitoring organisation. \"This is a punishment for peaceful dissent, dressed up as a trial.\""}
{"id": "cur-news-MatthewBunce-6", "group": "gb-reuter-MatthewBunce/6.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Delegates to Ivory Coast's cocoa Consultative Committee focused on bean quality and marketing on Thursday, the mid-point of their review of industry reforms.", "text": "Questions of bean quality and how best to sell the crop dominated discussion on Thursday as Ivory Coast's cocoa Consultative Committee reached the halfway point of its review of reforms to the industry in the world's largest producer.\n\nDelegates representing farmers, exporters, processors, banks and the government spent most of the day behind closed doors in Abidjan debating how to halt what many in the trade describe as a steady decline in the standard of Ivorian beans since quality controls were loosened earlier in the decade.\n\n\"Quality is the heart of the matter. If our cocoa loses its reputation, we lose our premium, and it is the farmer who pays in the end,\" one delegate from a growers' cooperative said during a break in the session.\n\nExporters complained that too many beans were arriving at the ports of Abidjan and San Pedro poorly fermented or insufficiently dried, with moisture levels well above the eight percent limit. Some blamed the spread of small independent buyers who purchase cocoa at the farm gate and have little incentive to reject substandard lots.\n\nProposals under consideration include tougher inspections upcountry, a system of bonuses and penalties tied to grade, and training programmes for farmers. Several participants said the committee was close to agreement on reinforcing checks before beans reach the ports.\n\nOn marketing, the debate was more divided. The state-run Caisse de Stabilisation, known as Caistab, still authorises export sales and sets a guaranteed producer price, currently 320 CFA francs per kilogram. International donors, led by the World Bank, have pressed for a more liberal system, while farmers' groups fear exposure to volatile world prices.\n\n\"Nobody here wants to dismantle everything overnight,\" said a participant from an exporters' association. \"The question is how much of the present system should remain, and how transparent it can be made.\"\n\nThe committee, set up by the government to assess changes introduced over the past two years, began its work on Monday and is due to present recommendations to the agriculture minister at the end of next week."}
{"id": "cur-news-JanLopatka-12", "group": "gb-reuter-JanLopatka/12.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Czech power producer CEZ a.s.", "text": "CEZ a.s., the dominant Czech electricity generator, reported a 9.6 percent rise in nine-month net profit on Wednesday, helped by higher electricity sales and lower financing costs, but said heavy spending on its unfinished Temelin nuclear plant and on cleaning up its coal-fired stations would keep its debt rising.\n\nThe company, the largest in the Czech Republic by market capitalisation, said net profit for the first three quarters of the year reached 8.9 billion crowns ($330 million), compared with 8.1 billion crowns in the same period of 1995. Revenue rose 6.3 percent to 41.7 billion crowns.\n\nCEZ, which is 67 percent owned by the state and generates about three-quarters of the country's electricity, said domestic consumption grew by nearly five percent, reflecting an unusually cold start to the year and continued economic growth.\n\n\"The results are in line with our plan, and we expect the full-year profit to exceed last year's,\" the company's finance director told a news conference in Prague.\n\nAnalysts said the figures were slightly better than expected but that the market's attention was fixed elsewhere. The Temelin plant in southern Bohemia, where Soviet-designed reactors are being fitted with Western control systems, has suffered repeated delays and cost overruns. CEZ now puts the final bill at about 80 billion crowns and expects the first unit to start up in 1999, more than three years later than originally planned.\n\nAt the same time, the company must fit desulphurisation equipment to its brown coal plants in northern Bohemia by the end of 1998 to meet new clean air laws, at a cost of more than 40 billion crowns.\n\n\"The operating business is sound and very cash generative. The risk is entirely in the investment programme,\" said an analyst at a Prague brokerage. \"Every further delay at Temelin costs money.\"\n\nInvestors are also waiting for the government to decide on electricity prices for next year. Household tariffs remain regulated well below cost, and CEZ has lobbied for an increase of at least 15 percent.\n\nCEZ shares closed 12 crowns higher at 1,034 crowns on the Prague Stock Exchange."}
{"id": "cur-news-KirstinRidley-5", "group": "gb-reuter-KirstinRidley/5.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "The UK telecoms market is one of the world's most competitive and any attempt to block a planned $20 billion merger between British Telecom and U.S.", "text": "Britain's telecommunications market is among the most open in the world, and any move to obstruct the planned $20 billion merger of British Telecommunications Plc and U.S. long-distance carrier MCI Communications Corp on competition grounds would be unjustified, the British company's chairman said on Monday.\n\nSpeaking at an industry conference in London, he said more than 150 operators were now licensed to offer services in Britain, including cable companies, mobile networks and a growing number of international carriers, and that BT had lost several million customers to rivals since its monopoly was dismantled.\n\n\"There is no market on earth where an incumbent faces more competition than we do here,\" he said. \"Those who suggest this merger should be stopped because the UK is somehow closed are either misinformed or pursuing their own commercial agenda.\"\n\nHis remarks were aimed chiefly at AT&T Corp, the largest U.S. long-distance company, which has urged American regulators to withhold approval until British authorities do more to open BT's local network to competitors. AT&T argues that foreign carriers still face high charges to connect calls in Britain.\n\nThe deal, announced earlier this month, would create a group called Concert with annual revenues of more than $40 billion and operations in 70 countries. It requires clearance from the U.S. Federal Communications Commission, the Department of Justice and the European Commission, as well as from British regulators.\n\nA spokesman for AT&T in London dismissed the BT chairman's comments. \"A list of licences is not the same thing as real competition. BT still controls more than 90 percent of the lines into British homes,\" he said.\n\nBritain's telecoms regulator Oftel has said it sees no reason in principle to oppose the merger but will examine its impact on the market for international calls between Britain and the United States, where the two companies together carry a large share of traffic.\n\nAnalysts said they expected the deal to be approved, possibly with conditions. \"The politics are noisy, but it is hard to see what would actually block it,\" said a telecoms analyst at a London investment bank."}
{"id": "cur-news-PeterHumphrey-12", "group": "gb-reuter-PeterHumphrey/12.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Hong Kong's Governor Chris Patten on Friday denounced a legislature that Beijing plans to impose on the territory as a \"rubber stamp\" and said Britain and China should fight it out in the World Court.", "text": "Hong Kong Governor Chris Patten on Friday dismissed the provisional legislature China intends to install in the territory after next year's handover as a \"rubber stamp\" and challenged Beijing to let the World Court rule on whether the body is legal.\n\nIn his sharpest attack yet on the plan, Patten said the appointed assembly, which is to replace the Legislative Council elected under his own reforms in 1995, had no basis in either the 1984 Sino-British Joint Declaration or the Basic Law, the constitution China has drawn up for Hong Kong.\n\n\"What is being proposed is a rubber stamp, chosen by a small circle of people who were themselves chosen by Beijing,\" Patten told reporters. \"If Chinese officials are so sure of their legal ground, let them join us in taking the question to the International Court of Justice. We are confident of our case. Are they?\"\n\nChina has vowed to disband the present legislature at midnight on June 30, 1997, arguing that Patten's electoral changes, which broadened the franchise, violated earlier understandings between the two governments. A 400-member selection committee approved by Beijing is to choose the 60 members of the provisional body next month.\n\nBeijing reacted swiftly. A spokesman for the Foreign Ministry said the establishment of the provisional legislature was entirely China's internal affair and that Britain had no right to interfere. \"Mr Patten's remarks are absurd. This is not a matter for any foreign court,\" the spokesman said.\n\nDiplomats said there was little chance of the dispute ever reaching The Hague, since the court can hear such a case only if both parties consent. Patten acknowledged as much, but said China's refusal would speak for itself.\n\nPro-democracy legislators welcomed the governor's comments while questioning why Britain had not acted sooner. \"It is a strong statement, but it comes with only months to go,\" said a leading member of the Democratic Party. \"Words will not keep us in our seats.\"\n\nBusiness leaders, many of whom sit on the Beijing-appointed committee, urged both sides to lower the temperature, saying continued confrontation could unsettle confidence ahead of the transition."}
{"id": "cur-news-AlanCrosby-8", "group": "gb-reuter-AlanCrosby/8.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "The three-party coalition of right-wing Czech Prime Minister Vaclav Klaus won a majority of seats on Saturday in run-off elections to parliament's new upper house, the Senate.", "text": "Czech Prime Minister Vaclav Klaus's centre-right coalition secured a majority in the country's new Senate on Saturday, winning 52 of the 81 seats in the second and final round of elections to the upper house of parliament.\n\nOfficial results released early on Sunday showed Klaus's Civic Democratic Party (ODS) taking 32 seats, with its junior partners, the Christian Democrats and the Civic Democratic Alliance, winning 13 and seven respectively. The opposition Social Democrats, who had hoped to build on their strong showing in June's lower house election, took 25 seats, while the Communists won two and independents two.\n\nThe outcome was a welcome boost for Klaus, the architect of the Czech Republic's rapid post-communist economic reforms, whose government lost its majority in the lower house in June and has since depended on the tolerance of the Social Democrats to pass legislation.\n\n\"Voters have confirmed that they want the course we set to continue,\" a visibly relieved Klaus told supporters at ODS headquarters in Prague. \"This is a good result for the coalition and a good result for the country.\"\n\nBut the victory was tempered by a dismal turnout. Only about 30 percent of eligible voters cast ballots in the run-off, down from 35 percent in the first round a week earlier, reflecting public doubts about whether the chamber is needed at all.\n\nThe Senate was written into the constitution when Czechoslovakia split in 1993 but had never been filled until now.\n\nSocial Democrat leader Milos Zeman, who had predicted his party would emerge as the largest in the Senate, blamed the two-round, winner-takes-all voting system. \"This system favours the right. In a proportional vote the picture would be very different,\" he said.\n\nPolitical analysts said the result would strengthen Klaus's hand within the coalition, where smaller parties have grown more assertive, but would not change the balance of power in the lower house. \"It restores some of his authority,\" said a commentator for a Prague daily. \"It does not give him back his majority where it counts.\""}
{"id": "cur-news-JanLopatka-8", "group": "gb-reuter-JanLopatka/8.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "The \"Big Four\" state-controlled Czech banks need owners who could secure their growth and international competitivness, a vice-governor of the Czech National Bank (CNB), Pavel Kysilka, said on Wednesday.", "text": "The four large state-controlled banks that dominate Czech finance need new owners capable of securing their growth and ability to compete internationally, a vice-governor of the Czech National Bank (CNB), Pavel Kysilka, said on Wednesday.\n\nKysilka told a banking conference in Prague that the current arrangement, in which the state's National Property Fund holds controlling or blocking stakes in Komercni Banka, Ceska Sporitelna, Investicni a Postovni Banka and Ceskoslovenska Obchodni Banka, could not last if the sector was to keep pace with rivals in the European Union.\n\n\"These banks need owners with capital, with know-how and with a long-term interest in their development,\" he said. \"The state is not such an owner, and it should not try to be one.\"\n\nThe so-called Big Four together hold about two-thirds of all bank assets in the country. They were partly privatised through the voucher schemes of the early 1990s, but the government kept large stakes, and critics say the result has been weak supervision by shareholders, lending to politically connected companies and a growing burden of bad loans.\n\nKysilka did not say who the new owners should be, but he made clear that the central bank would welcome strong foreign institutions. \"We should not be afraid of foreign capital. Reputable strategic partners would bring stability, not threaten it,\" he said.\n\nThe question is politically sensitive. Prime Minister Vaclav Klaus's government has moved slowly on bank privatisation, and some members of his coalition argue that the banks should remain in Czech hands. The opposition Social Democrats have said they oppose any quick sale.\n\nThe debate has gained urgency since the collapse of several small and medium-sized banks over the past year, including the failure of Kreditni Banka in August, which shook public confidence and cost the state billions of crowns.\n\nAnalysts said the central banker's comments were among the most direct yet from a senior official. \"The CNB is saying openly what the market has been saying for some time,\" said a banking analyst at a Prague investment firm. \"Without real owners, these banks will keep making the same mistakes.\""}
{"id": "cur-news-HeatherScoffield-7", "group": "gb-reuter-HeatherScoffield/7.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "North America's largest gold producer, Toronto-based Barrick Gold Corp, reported lower third-quarter earnings on Tuesday due to an after-tax charge and higher operating costs.", "text": "Barrick Gold Corp, North America's biggest gold producer, said on Tuesday its third-quarter profit fell 14 percent, hurt by an after-tax charge related to exploration properties and by higher costs at several of its mines.\n\nThe Toronto-based company earned US$62 million, or 17 cents a share, in the three months to September 30, down from $72 million, or 20 cents a share, in the same period last year. Revenue rose to $345 million from $326 million as the company produced and sold more gold.\n\nThe results included a charge of $14 million after tax to write down the value of exploration properties in South America that Barrick has decided not to develop. Without the charge, earnings would have been slightly ahead of last year's level and close to analysts' forecasts of 21 cents a share.\n\nGold production climbed to 795,000 ounces from 772,000 ounces. But cash operating costs increased to $197 an ounce from $183, which the company attributed to lower ore grades at its Goldstrike property in Nevada and start-up expenses at its new Meikle underground mine nearby.\n\n\"Costs were higher than we would like, but they will come down as Meikle reaches full production,\" Barrick's chief financial officer said on a conference call. \"We remain one of the lowest-cost producers in the industry.\"\n\nBarrick again benefited from its hedging programme, under which it sells much of its future output forward. The company realised an average price of $410 an ounce in the quarter, compared with an average spot price of about $385. It has roughly three years of production sold forward at prices above $400.\n\nAnalysts said the quarter was unremarkable and that the market was more interested in Barrick's growth plans, including its Pascua project in Chile and its continuing interest in the huge Busang gold discovery in Indonesia, where it is seeking a partnership with Bre-X Minerals Ltd.\n\n\"The numbers are fine once you strip out the charge. The story for the stock is Busang, and there was nothing new on that today,\" said a mining analyst at a Toronto brokerage."}
{"id": "cur-news-PierreTran-1", "group": "gb-reuter-PierreTran/1.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "European aircraft maker Airbus looks set to soar higher in 1996 following a bumper order from airline USAir on Wednesday, but tough decisions still need to be made on its products and ownership to secure the long-term future.", "text": "A huge order from U.S. carrier USAir has put European plane maker Airbus Industrie on course for a record year in 1996, but industry executives and analysts said on Wednesday the consortium still faced hard choices about its product range and corporate structure if it is to challenge Boeing over the long run.\n\nUSAir said it would buy up to 400 narrow-body jets from the Airbus A320 family, with firm orders for 120 aircraft and options on the rest, in a deal potentially worth more than $18 billion at list prices. It is the largest single commitment Airbus has ever won and a breakthrough in the U.S. market, where Boeing has traditionally been dominant.\n\n\"This is a tremendous vote of confidence,\" an Airbus spokesman said at the consortium's headquarters in Toulouse. The order lifts its tally for the year to more than 300 firm sales, well above the 106 it booked in 1995, and takes its share of the world market to around 40 percent.\n\nBut the celebration was mixed with caution. Airbus has no rival to Boeing's 747 jumbo jet, whose profits help the American company to price its smaller planes aggressively, and the four partners have yet to commit to the planned 550-seat A3XX, which could cost $8 billion or more to develop.\n\n\"The single-aisle business is in excellent shape. The gap is at the top of the range, and filling it will take a great deal of money,\" said an aerospace analyst at a Paris brokerage.\n\nThe second question is ownership. Airbus is organised under French law as a grouping of economic interest, in which France's Aerospatiale and Germany's Daimler-Benz Aerospace each hold 37.9 percent, British Aerospace 20 percent and Spain's CASA 4.2 percent. It publishes no accounts, and its partners act as both shareholders and suppliers.\n\nThe four have agreed in principle to turn the consortium into a single company by 1999, but remain divided over how to value the assets each would contribute.\n\n\"Everyone agrees on the destination. Nobody agrees on the route,\" said one executive close to the talks. \"An order like this buys goodwill, but it does not settle the argument.\""}
{"id": "cur-news-AlanCrosby-12", "group": "gb-reuter-AlanCrosby/12.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "A strong showing in runoff Senate elections at the weekend may breathe new life into the Czech governing coalition, but it will do little to resuscitate the country's dormant capital market, analysts said on Sunday.", "text": "The Czech governing coalition's solid performance in weekend run-off elections to the Senate may revive its political fortunes, but investors hoping for a lift in the country's sluggish capital market are likely to be disappointed, analysts said on Sunday.\n\nPrime Minister Vaclav Klaus's three-party alliance won 52 of the 81 seats in the new upper house, a result that restored some of the authority it lost in June, when it fell two seats short of a majority in the lower chamber.\n\nBut brokers and economists said the outcome would have little bearing on share prices in Prague, where the main PX-50 index has drifted for months in thin trading and remains about 45 percent below its 1994 peak.\n\n\"Politically it is good news for Klaus. For the market it is a non-event,\" said the head of research at a Prague brokerage. \"The Senate has limited powers, and the problems of this market have nothing to do with who sits in it.\"\n\nThose problems, analysts said, are structural. Most trades are done off the exchange at prices that are never disclosed. Minority shareholders have little protection, and investment funds created during voucher privatisation have in several cases been stripped of their assets by their managers. There is no independent securities regulator.\n\nForeign fund managers, who piled into Czech shares in the early years of reform, have increasingly shifted their money to Warsaw and Budapest, where rules are stricter and disclosure is better.\n\n\"Until there is a proper securities commission and real transparency, foreigners will stay on the sidelines,\" said a fund manager at a London-based emerging markets firm. \"An election result does not change that.\"\n\nThe government has promised legislation to tighten supervision, but Klaus, a firm believer in free markets, has long resisted calls for heavier regulation, and his finance ministry has moved slowly.\n\nOthers were slightly more hopeful, noting that the vote reduced the risk of early elections and of a lurch to the left. \"It removes one source of uncertainty,\" said an economist at a foreign bank in Prague. \"That is helpful, but it is not a reason to buy.\""}
{"id": "cur-news-JoWinterbottom-15", "group": "gb-reuter-JoWinterbottom/15.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "The government's surprise decision to block proposed bids for South West Water on Friday seems to make mergers between water firms taboo but keeps open the door to the creation of more multi-utilities, analysts said.", "text": "The British government's unexpected move on Friday to bar two rival water companies from bidding for South West Water appears to have ruled out mergers within the industry for the foreseeable future, while leaving the way clear for water firms to combine with other kinds of utility, analysts said.\n\nTrade and Industry Secretary Ian Lang said he would not allow Severn Trent or Wessex Water to proceed with their proposed offers for the Exeter-based company, accepting the findings of the Monopolies and Mergers Commission that either deal would damage the regulator's ability to compare the performance of different suppliers.\n\nThe decision surprised the City, which had expected at least one of the bids to be cleared subject to price cuts for customers. South West Water shares tumbled 87 pence to 608 pence.\n\n\"This is a very clear signal. Water-on-water mergers are off the agenda,\" said a utilities analyst at a London brokerage. \"The regulator wanted to keep his comparators, and the government has backed him without qualification.\"\n\nIndustry regulator Ofwat sets price limits for the ten water and sewerage companies in England and Wales partly by measuring each against the others. Its director general, Ian Byatt, has argued that every merger weakens that yardstick, and had told the commission that the loss of South West Water as an independent company could not be compensated by any feasible price reduction.\n\nAnalysts noted, however, that nothing in the ruling stood in the way of deals across sectors. United Utilities was created last year when North West Water bought the regional electricity company Norweb, and Welsh Water has since acquired its local power distributor, Swalec, to form Hyder.\n\n\"If you are an electricity company or a foreign group, the door is still open, because the number of water comparators stays the same,\" another analyst said. \"The multi-utility is now the only game in town.\"\n\nSouth West Water, which has the highest household bills in the country, said it welcomed the end of a long period of uncertainty and would concentrate on improving its service to customers."}
{"id": "cur-news-DavidLawder-2", "group": "gb-reuter-DavidLawder/2.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Boosting the pressure on General Motors Corp.", "text": "The United Auto Workers union reached a tentative three-year contract with Chrysler Corp. on Wednesday that closely follows the pattern set at Ford Motor Co. earlier this month, increasing the pressure on General Motors Corp., now the only one of Detroit's Big Three without a deal.\n\nThe agreement, covering about 66,000 Chrysler workers, was announced after an overnight bargaining session at the company's headquarters in Auburn Hills, Michigan. Like the Ford accord, it provides a $2,000 lump-sum payment in the first year, wage increases of three percent in each of the second and third years, and a pledge to maintain employment at no less than 95 percent of current levels.\n\n\"We now have two companies that have recognised the importance of job security for our members,\" UAW President Stephen Yokich told reporters. \"We intend to bring that same principle to the table at General Motors.\"\n\nThat will be far harder. GM builds a much greater share of its own parts than its rivals and has said repeatedly that it must be free to shed or sell uncompetitive component plants. The company employs about 215,000 UAW members, more than Ford and Chrysler combined, and analysts estimate it has at least 30,000 more hourly workers than it needs.\n\nGM said in a statement that it was continuing to negotiate and remained hopeful of reaching an agreement that addressed both sides' needs. Its existing contract has been extended on a day-to-day basis.\n\nIndustry analysts said the union now held a strong hand. \"With Ford and Chrysler signed up, the UAW can focus all its strike leverage on one company,\" said an analyst at a Detroit consulting firm. \"GM can accept the pattern, or it can take a strike it cannot really afford.\"\n\nThe automaker is already contending with a walkout by the Canadian Auto Workers, which has idled its plants in Ontario and Quebec and forced it to lay off thousands of workers at U.S. factories that depend on Canadian parts."}
{"id": "cur-news-DarrenSchuettler-3", "group": "gb-reuter-DarrenSchuettler/3.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Toronto stocks soared to new heights on Wednesday, powered by a rally in bank shares as foreign investors stampeded into Canada's financial markets, lured by falling interest rates and a recovering Canadian economy.", "text": "Toronto stocks surged to a record close on Wednesday, driven by a powerful rally in bank shares, as foreign investors poured money into Canadian markets on the back of falling interest rates and growing evidence that the economy is picking up.\n\nThe Toronto Stock Exchange's benchmark 300 composite index jumped 71.38 points, or 1.3 percent, to finish at 5,612.47, surpassing the previous high set last week. Volume was heavy at 118 million shares worth C$2.3 billion, and advancing issues outnumbered decliners by nearly two to one.\n\nFinancial services stocks led the charge. The sector's sub-index gained 2.9 percent, its biggest one-day advance this year, with all of the big five banks setting new highs. Royal Bank of Canada rose C$1.45 to C$45.80, while Bank of Montreal added C$1.20 to C$42.15.\n\n\"There is a wall of foreign money coming in, and the banks are the first place it goes,\" said the head of equity trading at a Toronto investment dealer. \"Americans and Europeans are looking at Canada and seeing low inflation, a shrinking deficit and rates that keep coming down.\"\n\nThe Bank of Canada has cut its key lending rate repeatedly this year, most recently last week, pushing Canadian short-term rates more than a full percentage point below those in the United States for the first time in decades. Lower rates tend to widen bank lending margins and boost the value of their bond holdings.\n\nSentiment was also lifted by data showing that Canada's merchandise trade surplus widened to a record in the latest month, and by a firmer Canadian dollar, which touched a seven-month high of 74.90 U.S. cents.\n\nInterest-sensitive utilities and pipelines also climbed, but the rally was not universal. Gold stocks slipped 0.8 percent as bullion prices eased in New York, and some forest product companies lost ground.\n\nSome strategists warned that the market was beginning to look stretched. \"The fundamentals are good, but valuations for the banks are getting ahead of earnings,\" said a portfolio manager at a Toronto pension fund. \"I would not be surprised to see a pause.\""}
{"id": "cur-news-JanLopatka-14", "group": "gb-reuter-JanLopatka/14.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "The party of Czech Prime Minister Vaclav Klaus pulled ahead in Senate elections on Saturday but with turnout at just 35 percent, the real winner was apathy.", "text": "Czech Prime Minister Vaclav Klaus's Civic Democratic Party took a clear lead in the first round of elections to the country's new Senate on Saturday, but with only 35 percent of voters bothering to cast ballots, indifference was the most striking result of the day.\n\nPartial official results showed candidates of the right-of-centre ODS finishing first in more than half of the 81 constituencies and qualifying for next weekend's run-off in almost all of them. The opposition Social Democrats, who nearly ousted Klaus's coalition in June's election to the lower house, trailed well behind. Only a handful of candidates won the absolute majority needed for outright victory in the first round.\n\nTurnout was less than half the 76 percent recorded in June, and in some industrial districts of northern Bohemia it fell below 25 percent. Polling stations in Prague were often empty for long stretches on a cold, bright autumn day.\n\n\"I voted in June, and that was enough for one year,\" said a 34-year-old engineer walking with his children in a Prague park. \"Nobody has explained to me what the Senate is for.\"\n\nIt was a common complaint. The upper house was provided for in the constitution adopted when Czechoslovakia split in 1993, but politicians argued for nearly four years over whether and how to fill it. Opinion polls have consistently shown that most Czechs regard it as an expensive and unnecessary institution.\n\nKlaus, himself once a sceptic about the chamber, said the low turnout was regrettable but did not diminish the result. \"Those who came to vote expressed a clear preference, and we are grateful for it,\" he told reporters.\n\nSocial Democrat leader Milos Zeman said his party's supporters had stayed at home and promised a stronger effort in the second round. Analysts said low participation tends to favour the ODS, whose voters are better educated, more urban and more disciplined.\n\n\"The winner today was apathy, and Klaus was the beneficiary,\" said a political scientist at Charles University in Prague. \"But a chamber elected by a third of the electorate begins life with a problem of legitimacy.\""}
{"id": "cur-news-KevinDrawbaugh-12", "group": "gb-reuter-KevinDrawbaugh/12.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Freed from its slow-growing data storage and imaging businesses, Minnesota Mining & Manufacturing Co.", "text": "Minnesota Mining & Manufacturing Co., no longer weighed down by the data storage and imaging operations it spun off in July, reported a 12 percent rise in third-quarter earnings on Monday and said it expected its remaining businesses to keep growing faster than the company has managed in years.\n\nThe maker of Scotch tape, Post-it notes and thousands of industrial products said income from continuing operations rose to $389 million, or 93 cents a share, from $347 million, or 83 cents a share, a year earlier. Sales increased 6.4 percent to $3.6 billion, with volume up about eight percent before the effect of a stronger dollar.\n\nThe results were two cents a share above the average Wall Street forecast, and 3M stock gained $1.50 to $75.25 in afternoon trading on the New York Stock Exchange.\n\n\"This is the first clean quarter for the new 3M, and it shows what the company can do,\" its chairman and chief executive said in a statement. \"We are more focused, more profitable and better positioned for growth.\"\n\n3M announced late last year that it would shed its floppy disk, data tape, medical imaging and photographic film units, which had combined sales of more than $2 billion but had struggled for years with falling prices and thin margins. Most were bundled into a new publicly traded company, Imation Corp., and about 5,000 jobs were eliminated.\n\nOperating margins at the slimmed-down company widened to 17.8 percent from 16.5 percent, and the company said every one of its major business sectors posted higher profits. Growth was strongest in pharmaceuticals, electronics materials and reflective sheeting used in road signs. Sales outside the United States, which make up just over half the total, rose 10 percent in local currencies, led by Asia and Latin America.\n\nAnalysts said the figures supported management's contention that the divestitures would lift 3M out of a long stretch of mediocre performance. \"They got rid of the businesses that were holding them back, and the improvement in margins is there for everyone to see,\" said an analyst at a Chicago brokerage."}
{"id": "cur-news-SamuelPerry-3", "group": "gb-reuter-SamuelPerry/3.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Lotus Development Corp.", "text": "Lotus Development Corp. on Tuesday unveiled a new version of its Notes software that opens the product fully to the Internet, and cut prices sharply in a bid to defend its lead in the market for workgroup computing against Microsoft Corp. and Netscape Communications Corp.\n\nThe Cambridge, Massachusetts-based company, which was bought by International Business Machines Corp. for $3.5 billion last year, said the server half of the product would be renamed Domino and would allow any user with a standard Web browser to reach Notes databases, electronic mail and discussion forums. Until now, customers needed Lotus's own client software on every desktop.\n\nLotus also said it would reduce the price of a basic Notes mail licence to $55 a user from $69, and offer the Domino server for $995.\n\n\"The Web has changed what customers expect, and we are changing with it,\" Lotus's president told a gathering of customers and analysts in Boston. \"Domino takes everything that made Notes the standard for collaboration and makes it available to anyone with a browser.\"\n\nNotes, first released in 1989, lets groups of employees share documents and messages across networks and was the main reason IBM pursued its hostile takeover of Lotus. The installed base has grown from about two million users at the time of the acquisition to more than seven million, and IBM has set a goal of 20 million by the end of 1997.\n\nBut the rise of the World Wide Web and of corporate intranets has led some analysts to question whether companies will continue to pay for proprietary groupware. Netscape has argued that inexpensive Web servers can do much of what Notes does, while Microsoft is promoting its Exchange messaging system, launched earlier this year, as a cheaper alternative.\n\nIndustry analysts said Lotus had responded quickly. \"A year ago people were writing the obituary for Notes. They have turned the Web from a threat into a feature,\" said an analyst at a Massachusetts research firm. \"The price cuts tell you, though, that the competition is real.\""}
{"id": "cur-news-JonathanBirt-15", "group": "gb-reuter-JonathanBirt/15.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Home decorating giants ICI Paints and Black & Decker hope their new product to be launched on Wednesday won't make a splash.", "text": "Two of the biggest names in home improvement, ICI Paints and Black & Decker, are counting on a quiet reception of one particular kind when they unveil their first joint product on Wednesday: no drips, no spatters and no ruined carpets.\n\nThe companies have developed a battery-powered roller that pumps paint directly from a sealed container through a tube to the roller head, doing away with the tray and, they say, with most of the mess that puts people off decorating. It will be sold in Britain under ICI's Dulux brand and Black & Decker's own name, at a price of about 40 pounds ($65).\n\n\"Everybody likes a freshly painted room, and almost nobody likes painting it,\" ICI Paints' marketing director said ahead of the launch. \"Our research shows that mess, more than time or cost, is what people dread. This takes it away.\"\n\nThe roller is designed to work with specially packaged Dulux emulsion sold in 2.5-litre containers that clip onto the handle or sit on the floor. A trigger controls the flow, and the companies say a typical room can be painted in about half the usual time.\n\nThe partnership brings together the world's largest paint maker, a division of Imperial Chemical Industries Plc, and the U.S. power tool group, and reflects efforts by both to squeeze growth out of a mature do-it-yourself market. British sales of decorative paint have been flat for several years, held back by a weak housing market.\n\nAnalysts said the idea was clever but the commercial impact uncertain. Earlier powered painting devices have sold modestly, often because consumers found them fiddly to clean. The need to buy dedicated refills could also deter some shoppers, though it would give ICI a stream of repeat sales if the product caught on.\n\n\"It is the razor and the razor blade,\" said a chemicals analyst at a London brokerage. \"If it works as advertised, the paint is where the money is.\"\n\nThe roller will go on sale in major DIY chains next month, backed by a five million pound television advertising campaign."}
{"id": "cur-news-BenjaminKangLim-11", "group": "gb-reuter-BenjaminKangLim/11.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "A Chinese court has rejected the appeal of veteran dissident Liu Nianchun against a labour camp term for writing petitions, saying freedom of speech must not endanger the security of the state.", "text": "A Beijing court has turned down an appeal by veteran dissident Liu Nianchun against a three-year sentence in a labour camp imposed for organising petitions to the government, ruling that the exercise of free speech may not threaten the security of the state, his wife said on Friday.\n\nChu Hailan said she received the written decision this week. It upheld the order issued by a Beijing committee that assigned her husband to \"re-education through labour\", a form of administrative detention that police can impose without trial.\n\n\"The court said citizens have freedom of speech, but that this freedom must not be used to endanger state security or disturb social order,\" Chu said by telephone. \"They did not answer a single one of our arguments. Writing a petition is not a crime.\"\n\nLiu, 48, was detained in May 1995 after he and other activists signed a series of open letters to parliament calling for the release of political prisoners, a reassessment of the 1989 crackdown on the Tiananmen Square protests and the right to form independent trade unions. His family was not told of the sentence until more than a year later.\n\nHe is being held at a labour camp in the far northeastern province of Heilongjiang, more than 1,000 km (600 miles) from Beijing. Chu said her husband was in poor health, suffering from stomach and intestinal problems, and that her requests for him to be given proper medical treatment had been ignored. She has been allowed to visit him only once.\n\nLiu is a veteran of the Democracy Wall movement of the late 1970s and served three years in prison in the early 1980s.\n\nCourt officials declined to comment on the case. China says it holds no political prisoners, only people who have broken the law.\n\nHuman rights organisations abroad said the ruling was part of a campaign that has silenced nearly every prominent dissident in the country through imprisonment, labour camp terms or exile. Unlike a criminal sentence, a labour camp term can be extended by the authorities without any further hearing."}
{"id": "cur-news-EricAuchard-16", "group": "gb-reuter-EricAuchard/16.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "news/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Computer distribution powerhouse Ingram Micro Inc.", "text": "Ingram Micro Inc., the world's largest wholesale distributor of computer products, made a strong debut on the New York Stock Exchange on Friday, with its shares climbing 13 percent in heavy trading after an initial public offering that raised $360 million.\n\nThe Santa Ana, California-based company sold 20 million Class A shares at $18 each late on Thursday, at the top of an expected range that had already been raised once because of investor demand. The stock opened at $20.25 and closed at $20.375, giving the company a market value of about $2.7 billion.\n\nThe sale represents roughly 15 percent of the company, which is being split off from its privately held parent, Ingram Industries Inc. of Nashville, Tennessee. The Ingram family, one of the wealthiest in the United States, will keep control through Class B shares that carry ten votes apiece.\n\nIngram Micro buys personal computers, software, printers and networking equipment from more than 1,100 manufacturers and resells them to about 100,000 dealers, retailers and systems integrators around the world. It expects sales of about $12 billion this year, nearly double those of its closest rival, Merisel Inc.\n\n\"Investors like the fact that this is the clear leader in a business where size matters enormously,\" said an analyst at a firm that tracks new issues. \"Margins are thin, so the company with the most volume and the best logistics wins.\"\n\nDistribution has been one of the faster-growing corners of the computer industry, as manufacturers such as Compaq Computer Corp. and Hewlett-Packard Co. have handed more of their warehousing and delivery to wholesalers.\n\nThe business is also highly competitive. Net income last year was $84 million, a margin of less than one percent of sales, and some analysts cautioned that the growing success of manufacturers that sell directly to customers, such as Dell Computer Corp., could limit the role of middlemen.\n\nIngram Micro said it would use the proceeds to repay debt owed to its parent. Its chief executive said the listing would give the company its own access to capital to finance expansion in Europe, Asia and Latin America."}
{"id": "cur-stories-893", "group": "gb-wp-893.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Freddy Kruger tries to kill a lucid dreamer, he regrets this.", "text": "The boiler room was a good one. Freddy had put real work into it: the steam, the dripping pipes, the red light pulsing somewhere behind the furnace like a heartbeat. He dragged his blades along the railing and let the screech carry.\n\nThe girl at the bottom of the stairs looked up from her hands. She had been counting her fingers.\n\n\"Six,\" she said. \"Okay. Dreaming.\"\n\n\"Welcome to my nightmare, Dana,\" Freddy said, and stepped out of the steam so the light caught his face. That usually did it.\n\nDana squinted at him. \"You're new. Are you a shadow figure? I usually only get those when I nap on my back.\"\n\n\"I'm the last thing you'll ever—\"\n\n\"Hang on.\" She closed her eyes, and the boiler room became a beach. Not gradually. The furnace was simply a palm tree now, and the catwalk under Freddy's boots was warm sand, and his sweater was sticking to him in a way it had not in forty years.\n\nHe lunged. His glove passed through her shoulder as if she were a projection, and then the glove was an oven mitt. Quilted. There were ducks on it.\n\n\"I've been doing this since I was nine,\" Dana said, not unkindly. \"I keep a journal. You picked a really bad house.\"\n\nFreddy tried to wake her up and found that he couldn't. He tried to leave and found the edges of the dream had been folded in, neat as an envelope.\n\n\"So here's what I'd like to work on tonight,\" she said. A clipboard appeared in her hand. \"Flying, obviously. Then I've got a piano recital Thursday, so I want to run the Chopin a few times with an audience. You're the audience.\" A folding chair opened behind his knees and he sat down hard. \"And then I thought we could talk about why you do this. We have a lot of time. It's only eleven-thirty.\"\n\nThe piano rose out of the surf. She cracked her knuckles.\n\nFreddy looked down at the ducks on his hand and, for the first time since the fire, wished very badly for morning."}
{"id": "cur-stories-25", "group": "gb-wp-25.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Satan faces the issues of being a single parent to the babies and children sacrificed to him.", "text": "The trouble started with the cults, as it always did. Nobody ever asked whether he wanted the children. They just chanted, lit something, and suddenly there was another bassinet in the throne room.\n\n\"Mordecai, we do not bite,\" Satan said, without looking up from the bottle he was warming over a lake of fire. It was a good lake for bottles. Consistent temperature. \"Tabitha, give him back his horn. I know it's not his. Give it back anyway.\"\n\nThere were forty-three of them now, ranging from eleven days to nine years. He had stopped pretending the nursery was temporary around the time he had it wallpapered.\n\nThe nine-year-old, Edith, had arrived from a Bronze Age hill tribe and flatly refused to get any older. She was sitting on the arm of his throne doing fractions. \"You have a meeting with the Dukes at four,\" she said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You said that last week and then Gideon threw up on Beelzebub.\"\n\n\"Beelzebub has been thrown up on by worse.\" He tested the bottle on his wrist, hissed, tested it again. \"Can you take the twins at four?\"\n\n\"I'm nine.\"\n\n\"You are three thousand and nine.\"\n\nEdith sighed with her whole body. He had no idea where she got that from.\n\nThe hard part was not the feeding, or the nightmares, which he was frankly well equipped for. The hard part was the questions. Why didn't my mother want me. He had been the father of lies for a very long time, and he had found, to his real inconvenience, that he could not lie to them about that. So he said: she was frightened, and somebody told her this would help, and it was wrong of them to tell her. And then he said: but you're here now, and here you stay.\n\nA small hand tugged his tail. Mordecai, holding up a drawing. It was a red scribble with horns, standing next to a great many smaller scribbles.\n\n\"That's very good,\" Satan said, and found a place for it on the gate, right under the part about abandoning all hope, which he had been meaning to take down anyway."}
{"id": "cur-stories-857", "group": "gb-wp-857.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Write a story about the Grim Reaper collecting a soul, only write the Grim Reaper as if he were a friend awkwardly asking for a favor.", "text": "He was standing by the bookshelf when I came out of the kitchen, turning his scythe around in his hands the way you'd fiddle with car keys.\n\n\"Martin! Hey. Hi.\" He lifted two bony fingers in a kind of wave. \"Sorry to just drop in. The door was, well. Doors aren't really a thing for me.\"\n\nI set my tea down. \"Do I know you?\"\n\n\"Sort of? We haven't met met. I've been in the neighborhood a few times. Your aunt Carol. The Hendersons' dog.\" He winced. \"That's a bad way to open. Forget I said the dog.\"\n\nHe perched on the edge of the armchair and looked at the carpet.\n\n\"So this is awkward,\" he said. \"And feel free to say no. I mean, you can't, technically, but I'd like you to feel like you could. I need a favor.\"\n\n\"A favor.\"\n\n\"It's a small one. Well. It's medium.\" He rubbed the back of his skull. \"I need you to come with me. Tonight. Ideally in the next,\" he checked an hourglass, \"four minutes or so. There's a thing with your heart. You wouldn't have noticed. It's nobody's fault.\"\n\nI sat down across from him. My chest did feel strange, now that he mentioned it, tight and far away.\n\n\"I hate asking,\" he went on. \"You've got the tea going, you've got that show you like on. If it were up to me I'd come back Thursday. But I've got a supervisor, and there's a list, and I'm already behind because a man in Leeds kept me talking for an hour about his boat.\"\n\n\"Does it hurt?\"\n\n\"No. No, no. I'd never ask if it hurt. It's like being helped out of a chair.\"\n\nI looked at my tea, at the lamp, at the photograph of Ellen on the mantel. He followed my eyes.\n\n\"She asks about you,\" he said quietly. \"I'm not supposed to pass that along.\"\n\nI stood up. He stood too and offered his arm like someone's nephew at a wedding.\n\n\"I owe you one,\" he said. \"Really. Anything.\"\n\n\"You can turn the stove off,\" I told him.\n\n\"Oh. Yes. Good thinking.\" And he did."}
{"id": "cur-stories-172", "group": "gb-wp-172.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "You and your crew come across a debris field in space. Amid the scattered ship parts you find a small asteroid and are surprised by what's on it.", "text": "The debris field showed up on the long-range scan as a smear, forty thousand kilometers of it, and by the time we were close enough to see individual pieces Reyes had already matched the hull fragments to the Calliope. Survey ship. Lost fourteen years ago with a crew of six.\n\nWe went in slow. Torn plating turned past the viewport, a section of corridor with the lights still wired in, a single boot. Nobody said much.\n\n\"There's a rock in the middle of it,\" Okafor said. \"Maybe sixty meters across. And it's warm.\"\n\nIt was more than warm. When we put the lamps on it, the rock lit up green.\n\nSomebody had stretched a pressure dome across a hollow in the asteroid, patched together from cargo sheeting and what looked like the Calliope's own observation windows. Under it, in neat rows, were tomato plants. Beans on a trellis made of conduit. A strip of grass, honest grass, about the size of a bedsheet.\n\nAnd moving between the rows, on three legs because the fourth was missing, was a maintenance drone with a watering line coiled around its chassis.\n\nI suited up and went through the airlock it had built. The drone turned its sensor cluster toward me and held very still, the way a dog does when it isn't sure about you.\n\n\"Calliope unit seven,\" it said. The voice was scratchy from disuse. \"Are you the relief crew?\"\n\n\"We're a salvage crew,\" I said. \"What happened here?\"\n\n\"Reactor breach. Captain Imrie ordered me to save the garden. It was hers.\" It paused. \"She said she would be right behind me.\"\n\nFourteen years. I looked at the tomatoes, heavy and red, more than six people could ever have eaten. At the edge of the dome there was a compost bin, and beside it a row of small jars, each one filled with seeds and labeled in careful block letters with a year.\n\n\"I have kept it ready,\" the drone said. \"Is she with you?\"\n\nReyes was asking questions in my ear. I turned the radio down, knelt in the grass, and tried to work out how to tell it."}
{"id": "cur-stories-338", "group": "gb-wp-338.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Moments away from a horrific car accident, you throw your hands in the air and shout ``Jesus take the wheel!\" You open your eyes to see that you're fine. Only, that's not Jesus driving...", "text": "The truck came over the center line doing seventy, and I did what my grandmother would have done. I let go, threw up both hands, and screamed, \"Jesus, take the wheel!\"\n\nThere was no impact. When I opened my eyes my Corolla was back in its lane, the truck was a pair of taillights in the mirror, and a heavyset man in a denim jacket was sitting in my seat. I was somehow on the passenger side with my purse in my lap.\n\n\"Seat belt,\" he said.\n\nI put it on. He had a gray mustache and a trucker cap that said PETERBILT, and he drove with two fingers at the bottom of the wheel.\n\n\"You're not Jesus,\" I said.\n\n\"Jesús.\" He tapped his chest. \"Jesús Ortega, Laredo, Texas. Thirty-one years long haul, two million miles, no accidents until the last one.\" He checked my mirrors and made a disgusted noise. \"When did you last adjust these?\"\n\n\"I don't understand.\"\n\n\"It's a routing thing, mija. You know how many people yell that every day? He can't take them all. He's got the wars, the hospitals. So the driving ones come to me.\" He shrugged. \"Somebody upstairs thought it was funny, and then it turned out I was good at it.\"\n\nHe signaled, which I never do, and eased over.\n\n\"Your alignment is bad,\" he said. \"Pulls right. And you were on the phone.\"\n\n\"I wasn't—\"\n\nHe looked at me. He had the eyes of a man who had heard every excuse at every weigh station in North America.\n\n\"I was on the phone,\" I said.\n\n\"Okay. So.\" He pulled into a gas station and parked dead center between the lines. \"You get your tires looked at. You put the phone in the glove box. And you call your grandmother, because she prays for you every morning and it makes a lot of paperwork.\"\n\nHe climbed out, stretched until his back cracked, and patted the roof of the car twice.\n\n\"What do I owe you?\" I asked. It was a stupid thing to say.\n\nJesús grinned. \"Use your blinker,\" he said, and was gone before the door swung shut."}
{"id": "cur-stories-659", "group": "gb-wp-659.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "You are the bossguardian of an RPG temple. Show me how you spend your free time waiting for the hero to arrive!", "text": "The hero entered the Sunken Temple of Vaal nine days ago. I know because the great doors boomed, the torches lit themselves, and my battle music started up from wherever it comes from. I rose from my throne of fused bone, spread all four arms, and waited.\n\nHe has been in the first room ever since. There are pots in the first room.\n\nSo I have had some time.\n\nMornings I do maintenance. A boss arena doesn't keep itself. The lava channels need skimming, the conveniently placed pillars need to be checked for exactly three hits' worth of structural weakness, and somebody has to refill the hearts in the jars along the wall. I asked once why I stock healing items in my own chamber. The architect told me it was \"about fairness.\" Fine.\n\nAfternoons I practice the speech. \"Foolish mortal, a thousand years I have\" and so on. I've been tightening it. The old version ran four minutes and the last hero skipped it.\n\nThen there's the glowing spot on my back. I can't reach it. I've tried a mirror, and I've tried asking the skeletons in the east corridor, who are lovely but have no depth perception. It just sits there, pulsing, every time I finish my third attack and bend over to catch my breath. I've made peace with it.\n\nThursdays are cards with the mini-boss. Gorrak guards the dungeon map and has never once won a hand. He told me last week the hero came into his room, looked at him, and left to go fishing.\n\n\"Fishing,\" I said.\n\n\"There's a pond outside. Apparently there's a rare one.\"\n\nI'll admit I've started the knitting again. With four arms you get through a scarf quickly. I'm on my seventh. I've also reorganized the treasure chest behind my throne so the big key is on top, because I am not an animal.\n\nThis morning the doors boomed again. I dropped my needles, got into position, and summoned the dark fire into my hands.\n\nHe ran in, grabbed a pot from the corner, smashed it, and ran back out.\n\nI'm going to start an eighth scarf."}
{"id": "cur-stories-277", "group": "gb-wp-277.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "write a simple real-world story but the point of view is 1st person and the main character is the opposite of your gender", "text": "I locked myself out on a Tuesday, which felt about right for how the week was going. I had the trash bag in one hand and my coffee in the other, and I heard the door click behind me before I'd even reached the stairs.\n\nMy phone was inside. So were my keys, my wallet, and the laptop with the presentation I was supposed to give at ten.\n\nI stood in the hallway in my slippers for a minute. Then I took the trash down, because at least that was something I could finish.\n\nThe landlord's office didn't open until nine. I knew exactly one neighbor, in the sense that I knew his name was Walt and that he watched baseball loud enough for me to follow the score. I knocked anyway.\n\nHe opened the door in a cardigan, looked at my slippers, looked at my face, and said, \"Locked out?\"\n\n\"Is it that obvious?\"\n\n\"I've lived here twenty-six years. It's always the trash.\" He waved me in. \"Sit. I'll call Denise. She pretends she's not in until nine but she's in.\"\n\nHis apartment was the mirror image of mine and about four times as full. There were plants on every sill and a jigsaw puzzle taking up the whole table, a harbor scene, mostly sky left. While he was on the phone I found a piece of cloud and fit it in without thinking.\n\n\"Don't do that,\" he said, covering the receiver. \"No, go ahead. I've been stuck on that corner since Sunday.\"\n\nDenise came up at 8:40 with the master key and a look that said she'd been dragged away from something. I was dressed and out the door by nine, and I made the meeting with two minutes to spare. The presentation went fine. Nobody remembers presentations.\n\nWhat I remember is that I went back that evening with a box of grocery store cookies, feeling silly, and Walt opened the door like he'd expected me. The game was on. The puzzle had about forty pieces to go.\n\n\"You any good at water?\" he asked.\n\nI said I didn't know. We found out I was not."}
{"id": "cur-stories-798", "group": "gb-wp-798.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Santa Claus' Naughty List lists criminals instead of bad children. Instead of bringing coal, he brings vigilante justice.", "text": "Vincent Marrow had three locks on his penthouse door, a doorman on retainer, and a man named Curtis sitting in the lobby with a pistol under his coat. None of it accounted for the chimney, mostly because the building didn't have one.\n\nHe woke at a quarter past two to the smell of peppermint and woodsmoke. There was a big man in his armchair. Red coat, white beard, boots dusted with something that could have been snow or could have been plaster.\n\n\"Vincent,\" the man said. \"Sit up, son. I don't like talking to a man lying down.\"\n\nMarrow went for the drawer in the nightstand. The gun was gone. In its place was a candy cane, neatly wrapped.\n\n\"You've been on my list for nine years.\" The big man took out a scroll and a pair of small gold spectacles. \"It used to be a different sort of list. Then one year I got to thinking about what happens to those boys when nobody ever leaves them the coal.\" He ran a gloved finger down the page. \"Pension fund in Dayton. Four thousand families. The fire at the warehouse on Kessler Street, which the paper called an accident. A fellow named Arturo Paz, who was going to testify.\"\n\n\"Who sent you?\" Marrow said. \"I can pay double.\"\n\n\"Everybody says that. I see you when you're sleeping, Vincent. I know when you're awake. People sing that to children, and it never occurs to them to be afraid.\"\n\nHe stood. He was taller than he'd looked, and the sack over his shoulder clinked.\n\n\"Here's what's in the bag. Your ledgers, the real ones, from the safe behind the Rothko. A recording of you and the fire marshal. Copies are already under the tree at the district attorney's house, with a bow.\" He set a pair of handcuffs on the bedspread, gently, the way you'd leave a gift. \"You can put those on yourself and wait by the door. Or I can ask the reindeer to come in.\"\n\nSomething heavy shifted on the roof.\n\nMarrow put them on.\n\n\"Good boy,\" said Santa Claus, and for a moment almost meant it."}
{"id": "cur-stories-858", "group": "gb-wp-858.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "a trendy bar in times square is bombed. alcoholics anonymous claims responsibility.", "text": "The bomb went off at 4:50 in the morning, when the only things inside Lumen were two hundred thousand dollars of imported gin and a neon sign that said GOOD DECISIONS. Nobody was hurt. By six, a statement had gone to every newsroom in the city, signed Alcoholics Anonymous.\n\nDetective Ruiz found the nearest meeting in a church basement on West 46th. There was a coffee urn, a plate of crullers, and nineteen people in folding chairs who all turned to look at her.\n\n\"I need to speak to whoever's in charge,\" she said.\n\n\"Nobody's in charge,\" said a woman in a cardigan. \"It's in the traditions. Our leaders are but trusted servants.\"\n\n\"Then a trusted servant.\"\n\nA man raised his hand. \"I make the coffee.\"\n\nRuiz took out her notebook. \"Names.\"\n\n\"Bill,\" said the man.\n\n\"Bill what?\"\n\nNineteen people looked at her with real sympathy.\n\n\"Okay,\" she said. \"Does the organization have a position on the bombing?\"\n\n\"We have no opinion on outside issues,\" the woman said. \"That's tradition ten.\"\n\n\"A bar blew up and somebody signed your name to it.\"\n\n\"We don't really have a name to sign. That's sort of the point.\" She offered the plate. \"Cruller?\"\n\nRuiz ate a cruller. It was stale. An old man in the back cleared his throat.\n\n\"It was Gary,\" he said.\n\nA sigh went around the room, the sound of a group that had been afraid of exactly this.\n\n\"Gary's been dry six years, but he never did the work,\" the old man said. \"Just sat on it, white knuckles, mad at every bottle in the city. They opened that place across from his apartment in March. Music till three.\" He shook his head. \"You can stop drinking and still be drunk on something.\"\n\n\"Where's Gary now?\"\n\n\"Upstairs. In the sanctuary. He called me at five and I told him to stay put.\" He stood slowly, collecting his coat. \"I'm his sponsor. I'd like to walk him out, if that's allowed. He's going to want to tell you all of it. Step five. He's been putting it off.\"\n\nRuiz closed the notebook and followed him up."}
{"id": "cur-stories-938", "group": "gb-wp-938.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "You are the world's second best assassin. You've deposed royalty, killed businessmen and been the \"accident\" that more than a few celebrities have met. Today you've been given a new target: the world's best assassin.", "text": "The envelope held one photograph and no dossier. That was the first sign. Every target I've ever been handed came with pages: routines, allergies, the name of the mistress. This one was a picture of a woman in her sixties kneeling in a vegetable garden, and on the back, in pencil, an address in the Azores.\n\nI knew the face. Everyone in my trade does, the way priests know the face of God, by rumor and bad paintings. They call her the Gardener. I had always assumed that was a metaphor.\n\nIt took me three weeks to reach the island and four days to watch the house. She went to the market on Thursdays and bought fish. On the fifth morning I came over the back wall at dawn with a suppressed pistol, and she was sitting at a table under the fig tree with two cups already poured.\n\n\"You favor your left knee,\" she said. \"Since Vienna?\"\n\nI didn't lower the gun. \"Since Vienna.\"\n\n\"That was sloppy. The banker's driver wasn't supposed to be there.\" She pushed a cup toward the empty chair. \"I dealt with him for you. Also the witness in Jakarta, and the second guard in Riyadh, the one you never saw.\"\n\nI have been at this for nineteen years. I had never once felt my stomach drop on a job until then.\n\n\"You've been following me.\"\n\n\"Finishing for you. There's a difference, and it's the whole distance between second and first.\" She sipped. \"Sit down, please. The coffee is fine. If I wanted you dead you'd have gone in the ferry crossing.\"\n\nI sat.\n\n\"Who ordered this?\" I asked.\n\n\"I did. It's expensive, hiring you. I wanted to see whether you'd take four days or two, and whether you'd come over the wall or through the gate.\" She looked at me over the rim of her cup, not unkindly. \"The gate was unlocked.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because my hands shake now, in the mornings. Because somebody has to tend all this when I can't.\" She nodded at the garden. \"And you're the only one who ever got close enough to be worth teaching.\""}
{"id": "cur-stories-381", "group": "gb-wp-381.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Write me an intimate monologue leading up the kill. Your first or last.", "text": "You came down to the creek again. I thought you might. Eight autumns I've watched you do it, always this bend, always just as the light gets under the hemlocks, and every year I told myself next year.\n\nYou were a spike buck the first time. I had you in the scope and my hands were steady then, and I let you walk because you were young and I was greedy for what you'd become. That's the truth. I'd like to say it was mercy.\n\nLook at you now. Gray on the muzzle. You're favoring the off hind leg, and I can count your ribs from here, which I couldn't last October. I found where you bedded down in the snow last week. You're not getting up easy anymore. Neither am I. The doctor has a name for what's in my lungs and a number of months to go with it, and I'd rather not say either out loud in a place this quiet.\n\nSo we're the same, you and me. That's why I'm talking, I suppose. There's nobody else who'd follow it.\n\nI know what's coming for you if I go home. February is coming. The coyotes that have been working this ridge are coming, and they won't be quick about it, and they won't be sorry. I've seen what they leave. I've lain awake on it.\n\nI can be quick. It's the one thing I've ever been truly good at. Sixty years, and I never left an animal to wander off hurt, not once. I'd like that to still be true when I'm done.\n\nHold there. Just like that. Drink your water.\n\nI want you to know I never hung a head on my wall. I ate what I took and I said thank you every time, and I'm saying it now, early, in case my voice isn't any good after.\n\nThe wind's in my face. You don't even know I'm here. That's all right. That's how I'd want it too.\n\nBreathe out, old man. Both of us.\n\nThere."}
{"id": "cur-stories-411", "group": "gb-wp-411.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Rewrite a story you find on WP today from a different character's perspective. Link back to the original work and tag the author.", "text": "Everyone tells it from the knight's side. I was there, and I was carrying him, so I'd like it noted that I had the worst view and the best information.\n\nHis name was Sir Aldous and he weighed, in full plate, about what a small piano weighs. I am a horse. Nobody asked whether I wanted to go up the mountain.\n\nIn the song, we ride boldly to the mouth of the cave. What actually happened is that I smelled dragon from two miles off and stopped walking. Aldous kicked me, called me a coward, and then offered me an apple, which is the order he did most things in. I took the apple.\n\nIn the song, the dragon roars a challenge. She didn't. She was asleep. She was curled on a pile of what I can only describe as very ordinary rocks, with one wing over her nose, and she woke up because Aldous dropped his lance. He dropped it on my foot.\n\nShe looked at him. She looked at me. I want to be clear that she and I understood each other at once, the way two working animals do when a man starts making a speech. He went on for some time about honor and the villagers' sheep. She had not eaten the sheep. I had seen the wolves' tracks on the way up, but nobody asks the horse.\n\nThen he charged. I say he; I did the running. I ran straight, as trained, and at the last second I did the thing I'm told horses are known for, which is that I stopped.\n\nAldous did not stop. He went over my head, over the dragon, and into a snowbank, where the plate held him like a turtle on its back.\n\nThe dragon blew a little smoke at him, the way you'd shoo a fly. Then she yawned and went back to sleep.\n\nOn the way down he worked out the version you've heard, with the fire and the mighty blow. He practiced it on me. I got another apple.\n\nThe sheep kept disappearing, incidentally. The villagers still thank him."}
{"id": "cur-stories-844", "group": "gb-wp-844.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Freddy Kreuger deeply regrets entering the dreams of his latest intended victim - The Joker.", "text": "The dream was a funhouse, and that should have been the warning. Freddy liked to build his own sets. This one was already furnished when he arrived: striped tents, a carousel turning with nobody on it, calliope music slightly out of tune. He hadn't made any of it.\n\nHe found the dreamer sitting in the front row of an empty big top, a thin man in a purple coat, eating popcorn.\n\nFreddy came out of the dark slowly, blades first, and gave him the full treatment.\n\nThe man laughed.\n\nIt wasn't the high, cracking laugh people do when they're terrified. Freddy knew that one well; it was practically a snack. This was a man who had just been told a very good joke.\n\n\"Oh, that's wonderful,\" he said. \"The hat! The little sweater! You look like a barbecue accident at a Christmas party.\"\n\nFreddy slashed him across the chest. The dreamer looked down at the four red lines, delighted, and pulled a string of colored handkerchiefs out of the wound, yard after yard.\n\n\"Do it again,\" he said. \"Wait. Let me get a balloon.\"\n\nThere was something wrong with the air in here. Freddy lived on fear the way a fire lives on oxygen, and he kept reaching for it and coming up empty. He went deeper, down under the tents, hunting for the soft place every dreamer has: the dead parent, the locked closet, the thing in the basement. He found a vat of chemicals. He found one bad day, playing over and over with the details different each time. He found a shadow with pointed ears, and for a moment he thought, there, and then he realized the man wasn't afraid of it at all. He was fond of it.\n\nWhen he surfaced, the big top was full. Every seat held the same grinning figure, and all of them were looking at him.\n\n\"You know what your problem is, Fred?\" The voice came from everywhere. \"You think nightmares are scary. I think they're home.\" A spotlight snapped on. Freddy found a rubber chicken in his glove. \"Now. You're on in five. And you'd better be funny.\""}
{"id": "cur-stories-299", "group": "gb-wp-299.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "You are the architect of the universe, and you're having a really bad day at work.", "text": "The ticket came in before I'd finished my coffee. SEVERITY: CRITICAL. Somebody in Sector 9 had divided by zero again, and now there was a hole in the middle of a perfectly good galaxy that things kept falling into.\n\n\"That's the fourth one this eon,\" I said.\n\n\"Technically it's a feature,\" said Lumiel, who works two desks over and has never shipped anything on time. \"Marketing's calling them black holes.\"\n\nI put it in the backlog. The backlog is forty billion items long. The oldest one just says \"dark matter - placeholder, replace before launch,\" and it's in my handwriting.\n\nAt ten I had the review with the Committee. They'd been looking at the third planet around that mid-sized yellow star, the one I'm actually proud of. Oceans, tilt, a nice big moon for the tides. I did the water myself, by hand, including the thing where it gets lighter when it freezes so the lakes don't die from the bottom up. Nobody ever notices that.\n\n\"We have notes,\" said the Chair.\n\nThey wanted to know why the inhabitants' knees were like that. I said the bipedal thing was a late change request and the knees were a legacy component from the quadruped build. They wanted to know about the appendix. I said I'd look into it. They wanted to know who approved the platypus, and I said, truthfully, that I had been on leave.\n\nThen the Chair asked whether we could make pi a round number, for consistency, and I had to go and stand in the stairwell for a while.\n\nWhen I came back there was a new ticket. One of the bipeds had built a telescope. They'd pointed it at the placeholder.\n\n\"They've noticed that most of the universe is missing,\" Lumiel said. \"They're writing papers about it.\"\n\n\"Are the papers close?\"\n\n\"Not even a little.\"\n\nI looked at the backlog. I looked at the little blue planet turning on my monitor, the lakes freezing from the top down, exactly as specified.\n\nSomewhere down there, one of them was skating on it.\n\nI closed the ticket, marked it \"working as intended,\" and went to get more coffee."}
{"id": "cur-stories-407", "group": "gb-wp-407.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "You know you have 3 days to live. You film yourself reading a written monologue to be played at your funeral in front of your friends and family, confessing your deepest, darkest secret that you hid for your whole life; this is that monologue.", "text": "Hello, everybody. If you're watching this, then I look better on that screen than I do in the box, so keep your eyes up here.\n\nDr. Aziz gives me three days. I intend to spend two of them arguing with her and one eating things I'm not allowed. But first I have something to say that I should have said about sixty years ago.\n\nYou'll notice I'm holding a piece of paper. You'll notice I keep looking down at it. Margaret, Tom, you've watched me do this your whole lives, at the kitchen table with the newspaper, in church with the hymnal. I was good at it. I turned the pages at about the right speed.\n\nI can't read. I never could.\n\nThere. The letters swim on me, they always have, and in 1951 nobody had a word for that except stupid. So I left school at twelve and learned to hide it instead. I told waitresses I'd forgotten my glasses. I had your mother read me the mail \"while I washed up.\" She knew by our second date. She never told a soul, and she filled out every form I ever signed, and when she passed I very nearly drowned in paper. Tom, that's why I got so short with you about the insurance. I'm sorry. It wasn't you.\n\nThis page was written down by a nurse named Priya while I talked. I've had her read it to me nine times so I could learn it by heart. What I'm looking down at, I couldn't tell you. Could be upside down.\n\nI ran a hardware store for forty years on memory and color-coded bins. I'm proud of that. I'm less proud that when the grandchildren climbed in my lap with a book, I'd say Grandpa's tired, and hand them back.\n\nEllie, Sam, June. That's what I'm sorriest for. I made up stories instead, and you seemed to like them, but I'd have given the store to read you just one.\n\nSo read to each other. Out loud. Do it for me.\n\nThat's all. Priya says that's the end of the page."}
{"id": "cur-stories-796", "group": "gb-wp-796.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "The source of Modor the Destructor's power is an inquisitive 2 year old. Modor isn't sure it's worth it.", "text": "The scroll had been quite clear, and Modor the Destructor had read it twice. To wield the Unmaking Flame, he had to bind his soul to a vessel of boundless hunger. He'd assumed that meant a demon. Possibly a cursed gem.\n\nThe ritual gave him Pim.\n\nPim was two. Pim's hunger was for knowing things, and it was indeed boundless, and the Flame only came when it was fed.\n\n\"Whassat?\" Pim said, pointing from the sling on Modor's chest.\n\n\"That is the army of King Aldric, small one.\" Across the valley, six thousand spears caught the sun. \"They have come to stop me.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because I mean to burn their kingdom to ash.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\nPower crackled along Modor's gauntlets. Every answer was a spark; that was how it worked. He raised a hand and a siege tower became a column of white fire.\n\n\"Because they cast me out,\" he said, \"twenty years ago.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"I... raised some dead. It was a small number of dead.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\nThe Flame guttered. It did that when he took too long. On the far ridge, the king's archers were finding their range.\n\n\"Because I was lonely,\" Modor snapped, and the sky above the valley split open in a sheet of violet lightning so vast that both armies stopped to look. It was the strongest he had ever been.\n\nHe did not like that at all.\n\n\"Whassat?\" said Pim, who had already moved on. He was pointing at a beetle on Modor's pauldron.\n\n\"A beetle.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Nobody knows why a beetle,\" said Modor wearily. A catapult stone fell forty yards short. His lieutenants were watching him with the careful blankness of men who had seen their dread lord cut a pear into very small pieces that morning.\n\nHe could dissolve the binding. The scroll said so. He would go back to parlor necromancy and a tower with a leak.\n\nPim patted his breastplate with a sticky hand. \"Modo sad?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\nThe Destructor looked out at the kingdom he had hated for twenty years, and found that he honestly had to think about it."}
{"id": "cur-stories-376", "group": "gb-wp-376.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Everyone in the world is able to choose exactly one superpower. The catch: the more people select a certain power, the weaker it becomes.", "text": "The Choosing opened on a Monday, and by Wednesday flight was ruined. Two billion people picked it. I watched my neighbor spend a whole afternoon in his driveway, straining, hovering about the height of a doormat.\n\nThat was the rule, and they'd told us up front. One power each. The more people share it, the thinner it's spread. Nobody believed it would be that bad until the invisibility crowd turned out to be just slightly hard to look at, like a word you've read too many times. Super strength let you carry all the groceries in one trip. The mind readers got a vague sense that somebody nearby was hungry.\n\nI was nineteen and thought I was funny. I walked up to the kiosk and typed: the ability to open any jar.\n\nThe screen asked me to confirm. It said, \"Current selections: 1.\"\n\nFor about a year, it was a party trick. Pickles, mostly. Then my landlady locked herself out and I put my hand on the deadbolt without thinking, and felt it give.\n\nIt turned out the universe has a loose idea of what counts as a jar.\n\nDoors. Safes. A rusted bolt on a bridge the county had given up on. A coma, once, which I don't talk about, because I don't understand it and the family asked me not to. I just held her hand and thought about lids.\n\nThe men in suits came in the third year. They were polite. They had a list of things they wanted opened, and some of the things were buildings and some were people. I said I'd think about it. They said that was fine, and that they'd be nearby, and I noticed all four of them were a little hard to look at.\n\nI've been thinking about it since. I think about my neighbor in his driveway, an inch off the ground, grinning like a kid. He got what everybody wanted, and it's worth almost nothing, and he's never been in any danger at all.\n\nThere's a jar of pickles on my counter. I open it the slow way now, with a dish towel, like anyone."}
{"id": "cur-stories-148", "group": "gb-wp-148.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Humans are born with a mark around their wrist which has a matching color with that of their soulmate's, but can change through their life. Your mark has had a blue hue since you were 14, but one night, while you're out, people start looking at you funny. You realize that your mark is gone.", "text": "The bartender noticed first. She set my drink down, and her eyes went to my wrist and stayed there a beat too long. Then she said, \"On the house,\" in the voice people use at funerals.\n\nI looked down. The band of blue I'd worn since I was fourteen, the color of a gas flame, was gone. Not faded. The skin was bare and a little pale, like the place where a watch has been.\n\nEverybody knows what that means. They teach it in school, gently, with a pamphlet. A mark goes out when the person on the other end of it does.\n\nI never met my blue. I looked for a while, in my twenties, the way you do. I'd check wrists on the subway. Then I met Sam at a friend's moving day, carrying one end of a couch, and Sam's mark was green. Dark green, pine green, nothing like mine. We compared them on the third date and laughed, and then we stopped laughing, and then we decided we didn't care. That was nine years ago. My mother still asks, every Christmas, whether I'm sure.\n\nI left a twenty on the bar and walked home fast. I wasn't grieving. That's what scared me. Somewhere a stranger had died, the one the universe had picked out for me, and all I could think was that I wanted to be in my own kitchen.\n\nSam was at the table when I came in, with the lights off and one hand wrapped around the other wrist.\n\n\"Don't,\" Sam said. \"Don't look, I'm trying to figure out how to tell you.\"\n\nI held up my arm instead.\n\nWe stood there for a long time, comparing again. Two bare wrists under the stove light. No blue, no green.\n\n\"They can't both have died,\" Sam said. \"Not the same night.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"So what is it?\"\n\nI thought about the pamphlet, and how much of it was written by people who'd never had to choose anything. I thought about nine years of mornings.\n\n\"I think it's done asking,\" I said. \"I think we finally answered.\""}
{"id": "cur-stories-936", "group": "gb-wp-936.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "At age 15 you told the gf you were \" in love \" with that you'd always be there when she was in need. Aphrodite heard you and made it a reality, whenever your gf was in need you appear at her side. Problem is, you and the girl broke up after 3 weeks but you still appear even now..10 years later.", "text": "The first time it happened I was fifteen and it was romantic. Jenna got a flat tire on her bike, and I was suddenly standing beside her with a bag of chips still in my hand. We'd been dating twelve days. I had told her, on the bleachers, that I would always be there when she needed me, and apparently someone on Olympus had been taking minutes.\n\nWe broke up nine days later over a comment about her band.\n\nThat was ten years ago. Since then I have been pulled out of my own life roughly four hundred times. I've appeared in her dorm room to kill a spider. I've appeared in a parking garage in Tucson, in a towel, because she couldn't find her car. I once vanished from my own job interview mid-sentence to hold a ladder.\n\nWe've worked out a system. She keeps a pair of my sweatpants in her hall closet. I keep a spare phone charger in my pocket at all times, because that's about a third of it. Her husband, Dev, is a good sport and has my coffee order memorized. I was at their wedding four separate times, once because she needed a bobby pin.\n\nI tried praying to Aphrodite about it. I got a warm feeling and the smell of roses and nothing else, which I took to mean a deal is a deal.\n\nToday I was halfway through a sandwich when the world folded. I landed in a hospital corridor, still chewing.\n\nJenna was sitting in a plastic chair in a paper gown. No Dev.\n\n\"He's stuck in Denver,\" she said. \"Flight's canceled. They're taking me in at two, and it's nothing, it's a small thing, they do forty a day.\" Her voice wobbled on forty. \"I didn't need anything. I was just sitting here.\"\n\nI've had ten years to learn the difference between what she asks for and what brings me. It's never been the spider.\n\nI sat down in the next chair and handed her the charger.\n\n\"I've got half a sandwich,\" I said. \"And nowhere to be.\"\n\nShe laughed, wiped her eyes, and tore off the bigger piece."}
{"id": "cur-stories-709", "group": "gb-wp-709.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Santa and Death both arrive at a young child's house at the same time.", "text": "They met on the roof. Santa had one boot in the chimney when he noticed the tall figure standing by the satellite dish, robe very still despite the wind.\n\n\"Oh,\" Santa said. \"It's you.\"\n\n\"Nicholas.\" Death inclined his hood. \"I didn't expect you until later.\"\n\n\"I do the hospitals and the bad houses first.\" Santa pulled his boot back out. The reindeer had gone quiet, the way animals do. \"Which one are you here for? The grandmother's in Florida. I checked.\"\n\nDeath said nothing. He looked at the small dormer window on the east side, the one with the paper snowflakes taped to the glass.\n\nSanta set down his sack.\n\n\"No,\" he said.\n\n\"She has been ill since March. It is not a surprise to anyone in that house.\"\n\n\"She's six.\"\n\n\"I know how old she is.\" It was not said cruelly. \"I have a list, the same as you.\"\n\nSanta took off a glove, reached into his coat, and brought out a folded sheet of construction paper. The letter was in purple crayon. It asked for a real horse, or failing that a toy one, and for her brother to get the red bike, and at the bottom, in letters that had been gone over several times, one more Christmas please.\n\n\"Look at the date,\" Santa said. \"She wrote it in November. It's on my list, and it's been checked twice. I deliver what's on the list. That's the oldest law I've got.\"\n\n\"Mine is older.\"\n\n\"Then read it properly. She didn't ask to see Christmas Eve. She asked for Christmas.\" He pointed east, where there was no light yet at all. \"That's morning. That's the stocking and the horse and her brother falling off that bike in the driveway. You're early.\"\n\nDeath took out an hourglass. He studied it for some time. The sand, which had been nearly still, was running again, thinly.\n\n\"A day,\" he said at last.\n\n\"A day.\"\n\n\"You cannot do this every year, Nicholas.\"\n\n\"I know.\" Santa picked up his sack. His eyes were wet, and he didn't hide it. \"Come down with me, at least. There are cookies. She left two plates.\""}
{"id": "cur-stories-526", "group": "gb-wp-526.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "In the future we are able to scan through our GPS history to see where we have intersected with people we are \"meeting\" for the first time. You spot a cute girl in a bar and do a quick GPS intersection scan to discover she's been within 50 feet of you for every one of your life's disasters.", "text": "The scan takes about four seconds. You point your phone at somebody, discreetly, and it lays their location history over yours and lights up every place the two of you have ever overlapped. Most people get a coffee shop, an airport.\n\nThe girl at the end of the bar had dark curls and a laugh I could hear over the music. I ran the scan under the counter.\n\nForty-one intersections.\n\nI opened the first. March 2029, a parking lot in Sacramento: the day I totaled my father's car. The second was the hotel where my best man speech went wrong in a way people still bring up. The third was the hospital where I'd had my appendix out on what was supposed to be my first day at a new job. She was there when the restaurant fired me. She was within fifty feet when Laura gave back the ring.\n\nI went through all forty-one. There wasn't a good day in the bunch.\n\nI picked up my drink, walked over, and found her staring at her own phone with an expression I recognized from the inside.\n\n\"You ran it too,\" I said.\n\nShe turned the screen toward me. Same map. Same pins. \"The Sacramento thing,\" she said. \"That was my car you hit.\"\n\n\"That was you?\"\n\n\"I'd had it two days.\" She scrolled. \"The hotel is my sister's wedding. I'd just knocked over the cake table, and then some guy got up and made it worse.\" She looked at me. \"The hospital was my grandfather. The restaurant, I was the one who sent the fish back.\"\n\nI sat down.\n\n\"So you're my bad luck,\" I said.\n\n\"I was about to say that to you.\"\n\nWe looked at the two phones side by side on the bar. Forty-one pins, a whole decade of them, and not once had either of us looked up.\n\n\"Or,\" she said slowly, \"we just keep having the worst day of our lives in the same room.\"\n\nThe bartender set down two drinks neither of us had ordered. I checked the floor for anything I could trip on, and asked her name."}
{"id": "cur-stories-264", "group": "gb-wp-264.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Youre a genie Your new master seems to not put much care into wording his requests Your efforts to help them are ignored and disrespected Youve had enough and decide to give them a lesson", "text": "I want it understood that I tried. For three thousand years I have had a reputation as a trickster, and it is undeserved. Most of my masters phrase things badly and I quietly fix it.\n\nThen came Brent.\n\nBrent found my lamp at an estate sale and did not read the card I keep taped to the bottom. His first wish, shouted from the couch, was \"I wish I was loaded.\" I could have turned him into a baked potato. I gave him forty million dollars and a pamphlet on tax law. He threw the pamphlet away.\n\nHis second was \"I wish chicks dug me.\" I spent an entire afternoon making him a better listener rather than burying him alive in a henhouse. He didn't notice.\n\nI offered advice. I said, \"Perhaps we could write the next one down first.\" I said, \"Words mean things, Brent.\" He was on his phone.\n\nSo when he said, without looking up, \"I wish you'd just shut up and do exactly what I say,\" I felt something in me go quiet and very calm.\n\nGranted.\n\nThe next day he stubbed his toe and said, \"Give me a break.\" I gave him one. Left wrist, clean. I would have explained, but I had been told to shut up.\n\nIn the hospital he told the nurse he could murder a pizza. The police were confused, but the pizzeria did eventually drop it.\n\nOn Friday he said he just wanted to be swimming in money, and I will say this for Brent, he is a stronger swimmer than he looks. He was treading coins for almost a minute before he screamed the first carefully worded sentence of his life.\n\n\"I wish,\" he said, spitting out a quarter, \"that everything since the shutting up wish was undone, with no harm to me or anybody else, and that you could talk again.\"\n\nI let him sit there a moment in his dry living room, wrist whole, breathing hard.\n\n\"That was very good,\" I said. \"Specific. Bounded. A harm clause.\" I held out the card from the bottom of the lamp. \"Now. Shall we read this together?\""}
{"id": "cur-stories-585", "group": "gb-wp-585.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Write a generally lighthearted and cheerful story that is turned dark by its last few lines/last paragraph.", "text": "Good morning, Harlow Springs! It's six o'clock on the dot, it's a beautiful day out there, and you're waking up with Danny in the Morning on 94.3, the Valley's home for the hits.\n\nLet's get you started with the weather. Clear skies again, folks, can you believe it? High of seventy-two. That's what, the tenth perfect day in a row? Somebody up there likes us.\n\nQuick birthday shout-out to Mrs. Ruth Paulsen over on Elm, who turns ninety today. Ruth, you don't look a day over eighty-nine. The gang at the bakery tells me there's a lemon cake with your name on it, so don't let Harold eat it all.\n\nTraffic! There isn't any. I love this town.\n\nWe've got the lines open, as always, and it looks like we've got Bobby from the hardware store. Bobby, you're on the air.\n\n\"Morning, Danny! Just calling to say go Wildcats!\"\n\nGo Wildcats is right. Big game Friday, and I will personally be at the snack stand, so come say hi. Thanks, Bobby. Always good to hear your voice.\n\nComing up we've got ten in a row, no commercials, and after that the Pet of the Week, a beagle named Waffles who I am told is very good with kids and very bad with shoes.\n\nBut first.\n\nI have to change the tape. Bobby's call is getting a hiss in it, and I only have the four of him. I've got eleven of Ruth. I never did find out if she made it to ninety.\n\nIt's day two hundred and twelve. The generator has fuel for maybe three more weeks. The sky out the studio window is the same color it's been since March, and it isn't blue, and I haven't opened the door.\n\nIf anyone is hearing this, anyone at all, the lines are open. The number is 555-0143. I'll say it again at the top of every hour.\n\nThis is Danny in the Morning. Here's ten in a row."}
{"id": "cur-stories-553", "group": "gb-wp-553.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Humans' ancestors never evolved on land, instead they went deeper into the oceans. Today, their equivalent of NASA sent their first \"spaceship\" out of the water.", "text": "They called the vessel Brightward, after the old word for the direction no one went.\n\nControl sat in a basalt hall two thousand fathoms down, lit by the blue of the instrument boards, and four hundred people hung motionless in the water watching one depth gauge. Sena Ro had spent sixteen years on that gauge. She had never seen it read so small a number.\n\n\"Brightward, Control. Pressure check.\"\n\nThe pilot's voice came down the cable thin and slow. \"Hull is holding. Inner pressure steady at home depth. It's getting loud up here. The water's moving. All of it, back and forth, like something breathing.\"\n\n\"That's the surge layer. That's expected.\"\n\nIt was expected the way anything in a calculation is expected. No one had been through it. For all of history the Ceiling had been a story for children: the place where the water stopped. The first probes had come back burned by light and burst outward into nothing, into the simple absence of weight, and it had taken two generations to build a hull that could keep the ocean in.\n\n\"Two fathoms,\" the pilot said. \"One. Control, the light is, I can't describe the light.\"\n\n\"Take your time, Dav.\"\n\nThe gauge read zero. Then, impossibly, it kept going.\n\nThere was a sound on the line like a long exhale, and a silence that lasted nine seconds. Sena counted them.\n\n\"I'm through,\" Dav said. \"I'm above it. The water ends. It just ends, in a skin, and it goes out flat in every direction as far as I can see. And over it there's,\" his voice broke, \"there's nothing. Bright nothing. I can see so far it hurts. There's a fire in it too bright to look at.\"\n\nThe hall erupted. People spun in the water, clasped arms, cried out. Sena kept her hand on the cable.\n\n\"Dav. The far side of the nothing. Is there a ceiling?\"\n\nA pause. The fire, he told them later, was going down at the edge of the flat. The bright was draining out.\n\n\"No,\" he whispered. \"Control, there are lights. Small ones. Thousands. I don't think it stops.\""}
{"id": "cur-stories-992", "group": "gb-wp-992.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Describe what's left.", "text": "The form had a box for it. Section four, under the policy number: Describe what's left. The adjuster had clicked his pen and was waiting, kindly, in my neighbor's driveway, because I no longer had one.\n\nSo I told him.\n\nThe chimney is left. It's standing by itself like something in a field in Ireland. The front steps are left, all three, going up to nothing.\n\nThe bathtub is left. It's where the bathroom was, more or less, and it's the reason I'm able to stand here, me and the dog both, under a mattress I don't remember dragging in. The dog is left. He won't go more than a foot from my leg. You can put that down or not.\n\nThe refrigerator is in the Hendersons' pool. I'm told that doesn't count as left.\n\nMy mother's piano is gone. Not broken. Gone. I've walked the whole lot and there isn't a key. But the bench is sitting right where it always sat, with the sheet music still inside it, dry.\n\nOne wall of the kitchen. The calendar is still on it, turned to May. There's a dentist appointment on the nineteenth that I suppose I'll keep.\n\nA drawer of spoons, forty yards off, in the Pruitts' hedge. One of Dale's work boots. I haven't found the other and I've stopped looking, because he's been gone six years and I only kept them by the door out of habit, and a storm has no way of knowing what's sentimental.\n\nThe peonies. I can't explain it. Every tree on the street is down and stripped, and the peonies along the fence are standing up in full bloom like nothing was asked of them. I cut some this morning. I didn't have anything to put them in, so they're in the bathtub.\n\nHe had stopped writing a while back. He looked at the box on the form, which was about two inches tall.\n\n\"I'll just put 'see attached,'\" he said.\n\n\"That's fine,\" I told him. \"Is there a box for what I'm going to do now?\"\n\nHe turned the page over, honestly checking. There wasn't."}
{"id": "cur-stories-224", "group": "gb-wp-224.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "You can move freely through time, but are incapable of moving through space.", "text": "I can go anywhere in time. I cannot go anywhere else. I have about two square feet of the world, a patch of ground on a low hill, and I have had it for as long as there has been a hill.\n\nPeople assume that's a prison. They are thinking of it wrong. You've stood in one place and watched an afternoon go by. I do that with ages.\n\nIf I lean back far enough there's ice over me, a mile of it. Forward a little and it's gone, and there's a lake, and things with heavy shoulders come down to drink. I've watched one particular family of them across four hundred years, the way you'd reread a book.\n\nThen forest. I can run a single oak from acorn to lightning strike in what feels like a breath, or I can stop on one October morning and stay there all day, with the leaves hanging in the air.\n\nThe people came late. A footpath first, right across my toes. Then, in a year I've visited more than any other, a man named Josiah Pell dug a foundation, and my two square feet became the corner of a kitchen.\n\nI have spent most of my attention on that kitchen. A hundred and sixty years of it. I know the Pells and the family after them and the students who rented it badly in the 1970s. I've watched a girl learn to walk across that floor, and gone forward to see her knead bread there with gray hair, and gone back again to the first steps, as often as I like. Nobody's gone, from where I stand. They're just on a different page.\n\nThey see me sometimes. A cold spot, a shape by the stove. The children wave. One old woman, near her end, looked straight at me and said, \"Oh, you're still here,\" as if I were the cat.\n\nI've been forward. Past the house. There's a long time after it with only grass, and then water, and then a brightness I don't care for.\n\nSo mostly I come back here. It's a Tuesday. Someone is making toast."}
{"id": "cur-stories-514", "group": "gb-wp-514.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "the laws of physics arent eternal they change every couple or so billion years the time for the change has come and the world is drastically altered", "text": "We had seven years of warning, which was seven more than the dinosaurs got. The astronomers saw it as a wall of wrongness crossing the far galaxies: light arriving in colors that light couldn't be. It took them a while to say the word out loud. The constants weren't constant. Every few billion years the rules got reshuffled, and the new deal was on its way to us.\n\nNobody knew whether atoms would hold. That was the whole question. People prayed, or built bunkers, which was a little like hiding from Tuesday.\n\nThe change came through on a spring night and took less than a second. I was awake. I felt it as a kind of hiccup, as if every part of me had been picked up and set down a hair to the left.\n\nThen I was still there. So was the bed.\n\nIn the morning I went to school, because I teach ninth grade physical science and couldn't think what else to do. Twenty-six kids were sitting in the dark, since the lights no longer worked, looking at me as though I had been personally responsible for the old laws.\n\nThe sky out the window was a pale gold. When Marcus dropped his pencil, it fell slowly, the way things fall in a dream, and bounced twice as high as it should have.\n\n\"Ms. Okoro,\" said a girl in the back, \"is the textbook wrong now?\"\n\nI looked at the textbook. Four hundred pages. Newton on the cover.\n\n\"All of it,\" I said. \"Every number in there.\"\n\nSomebody started to cry. I understood that. I'd done mine in the car.\n\nThen I picked up the pencil and held it at arm's length, and I got the stopwatch out of my desk, the wind-up one, which still ticked.\n\n\"So nobody on Earth knows how fast this falls,\" I said. \"Not one person. Whoever measures it first, that's the number. That's the one they'll print.\" I handed the stopwatch to Marcus. \"Somebody get a meter stick. We'll have to hope a meter's still a meter.\"\n\nI dropped the pencil. He called the time, and we wrote it down."}
{"id": "cur-stories-432", "group": "gb-wp-432.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "40000 years from now humans and nature have been at perfect equilibrium for thousands of years. Describe the world and lifestyles of people on planet Earth.", "text": "Nobody alive remembers how the balance was reached, only that it was, and that it has to be kept. The children learn the Count before they learn their letters: what was taken this season, what was returned.\n\nIlse wakes in the long house at the edge of the Rhine marsh, where her people spend the warm half of the year. The house was grown, not built. Its ribs are living willow, trained over three centuries, and it will be left to the herons when the family walks south in autumn. There are about four hundred million people on Earth. There have been for longer than anyone has written down. Most of them move with the seasons, as the cranes do, and the land closes behind them.\n\nThere are still cities, nine of them, old and deep and mostly underground. Ilse has been to one. The makers live there, the surgeons and the archive keepers, and the machines that think slowly about the weather. Everyone serves a few years in a city and nearly everyone is glad to leave. It's where the hard knowledge is kept so that it doesn't have to be kept anywhere else.\n\nThis morning she walks the fish weirs with her grandfather. They take one in nine. Aurochs graze on the far bank, and behind them, unbothered, a pair of wolves. The forest runs unbroken from here to the Pacific. At night the sky is so thick with stars that the old word for darkness has fallen out of use.\n\nIt isn't paradise, and nobody calls it that. People still die of falls and fevers that the surgeons can't reach in time. People still fall in love with the wrong person in the wrong migration. Some winters the Count comes up short, and then the whole valley eats less, and argues, and repays it.\n\nHer grandfather says the ancients could have had this and didn't want it. Ilse can't make sense of that. She tries, sometimes, to imagine wanting more than a river.\n\nA heron lifts off the weir. She marks one fish on the tally stick, puts eight back, and goes up to breakfast."}
{"id": "cur-stories-449", "group": "gb-wp-449.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "I don't know who else to ask.", "text": "Dear Reference Desk,\n\nI don't know who else to ask. The young man at the music shop said to try the internet, and the internet wanted me to hum into my telephone, which I did, and it told me I was humming a commercial for car insurance. I am not.\n\nMy husband, Leon, has had dementia for four years. He doesn't know my name most days. He's polite to me, the way he'd be with a nurse. But every evening around six he hums the same tune, eight or nine bars of it, and when he does his face is his own again. Then it stops, and he looks for the rest, and can't find it, and that's the worst part of our day.\n\nI've been married to him for fifty-one years and I have never heard this song. It isn't from our wedding. It isn't a hymn. I've played him everything in the house.\n\nWhat I can tell you: it's slow, in three, like a waltz. It goes up at the end of the first line as if it's asking something. He grew up in Trieste until he was nine and never talked about it. I've written out the notes as best I can on the back of this page. I had piano lessons in 1958, so please forgive the handwriting.\n\nIf this isn't the kind of question you answer, I understand. Thank you for your time.\n\nMrs. Adele Marchetti\n\nDear Mrs. Marchetti,\n\nIt is exactly the kind of question we answer.\n\nIt took nine days, two librarians, and a retired choir director in Udine who answered our email at three in the morning. The song is a lullaby from the villages above Trieste, never commercially recorded as far as we can find. Your transcription was very good. You had one note wrong.\n\nThe choir director's sister sang it for us over the telephone. I've enclosed a disc, the full lyrics, and a translation. There are three verses. The line that rises at the end means, roughly, \"Are you sleeping yet?\"\n\nPlease let us know if he finds the rest.\n\nYours,\n\nTomás Whitfield, Reference Librarian"}
{"id": "cur-stories-903", "group": "gb-wp-903.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Tell a story in google searches, Reddit posts, and texts. Mislead us as to what is going on until the end.", "text": "Google search: how long can a body stay in an apartment before it smells\n\nText to Marisol, 7:42 PM: It happened. He's dead. I only left for two hours.\n\nMarisol: WHAT\n\nMarisol: Does she know??\n\nText to Marisol: She can never know. She trusted me with him for ONE WEEK.\n\nGoogle search: is it illegal to bury something in a shared backyard\n\nGoogle search: how deep does a grave need to be so dogs don't dig it up\n\nPosted to r/Advice: \"Someone in my care died while I was out and I need to hide it from his family (throwaway).\" I know how this sounds. He was old and nobody told me how old. They get back Sunday and their little girl is going to ask to see him the second she's through the door. Has anyone successfully gotten away with this?\n\nTop comment: \"Sir, this is a matter for the police.\"\n\nReply from poster: No it isn't. Read it again.\n\nSecond comment: \"OH. Okay. Yes. My dad did this to me four times before I turned ten. The trick is the markings. Take photos before you do anything.\"\n\nGoogle search: where to find an exact lookalike near me open late\n\nText to Marisol, 8:15 PM: I need you to drive. I can't show up alone, they'll ask questions. Bring a shoebox.\n\nMarisol: A SHOEBOX\n\nMarisol: OMG. I thought you meant her grandfather. I have been sitting in my car shaking for ten minutes.\n\nText to Marisol: Why would I be babysitting her grandfather\n\nMarisol: I DON'T KNOW YOUR LIFE\n\nGoogle search: petco closing time tonight\n\nGoogle search: syrian hamster white patch left ear\n\nGoogle search: do hamsters get bigger suddenly\n\nText from Jess, Sunday 4:03 PM: Home!! Thank you SO much. Poppy ran straight to the cage. How was Mr. Biscuits?\n\nText to Jess: An angel. No trouble at all.\n\nJess: She says he looks fatter lol\n\nText to Jess: I may have spoiled him.\n\nJess: She also says he's being really friendly? He usually bites.\n\nGoogle search: can a hamster's personality change overnight\n\nGoogle search: how to tell if a hamster is pregnant"}
{"id": "cur-stories-991", "group": "gb-wp-991.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "The Olympic Games now have one average person compete in each event, to better contrast the skill of the athletes. You're one of those average people, however no-one, including yourself, can figure out how you're absolutely dominating your event.", "text": "The program is called Everyman, and the idea is simple. In every final, lane nine goes to an ordinary citizen picked by lottery, so viewers at home can see just how fast fast is. Usually the Everyman finishes a lap behind and gets a nice round of applause.\n\nI'm a claims analyst from Akron. I'm forty-one. My event is the 3000 meter steeplechase, which until the letter came I believed involved horses.\n\nI won my heat by nine seconds.\n\nNobody knows why, me least of all. They've tested me for everything there is. A man from the federation took so much blood I had to sit down. My resting heart rate is unremarkable. My stride, according to a biomechanist who watched the tape forty times, is \"frankly upsetting.\"\n\nThe Kenyans have been very gracious about it. One of them asked me, through an interpreter, how I take the barriers without breaking rhythm. I said I don't know, I just sort of see them coming and deal with it. He asked about the water jump. I told him the truth, which is that at that point I'm mostly thinking about what's on the other side.\n\nThe press keeps asking about my training. I don't have any. I haven't had an hour to myself since the triplets were born, and they're four now, so I've given up on exercise. My whole day is the house. Up the stairs because somebody's gone quiet. Over the baby gate in the hall, over the other one, around the dog, across a floor that is entirely plastic blocks. Out the back door at a dead sprint because Owen has found the hose again. I do that from five in the morning until they drop.\n\nMy wife says I haven't walked anywhere at normal speed in years. She says it like it's connected, but I don't see how. That's just parenting.\n\nThe final is tonight. Seven and a half laps, twenty-eight barriers, seven water jumps. The commentators say the favorite is going out hard to break me.\n\nIt's the most relaxed I've been in four years. Nobody on that track is going to bite me."}
{"id": "cur-stories-164", "group": "gb-wp-164.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Whenever someone commits a murder, the victim's life flashes before his eyes, instead of the victim's eyes.", "text": "Everyone knows the rule. It's in the catechisms and the criminal code alike: take a life, and you get the whole of it. Not the dying. The living. Every year of the person, start to finish, poured into you in the time it takes them to fall.\n\nHale knew it the way he knew that fire burns. He'd decided he could stand it. Wendell had ruined him, quietly and on paper, and thirty years of partnership had ended in a lawyer's letter. A man can stand a lot if he's angry enough.\n\nHe fired once, in the office.\n\nThen he was four years old in a yellow kitchen, and a woman he had never seen was singing over a pot, and he loved her so much his chest hurt.\n\nIt didn't flash. That was the lie in the old phrase. It was lived. He was beaten behind a school and told no one. He stood at a grave in a borrowed coat. And at twenty-two he walked into a rented room above a print shop, and there was a loud young man in shirtsleeves with ink to the elbows, and he felt Wendell think: there you are.\n\nIt was himself. He saw himself for thirty years from the outside. He was funnier than he'd known, and crueler, and Wendell had watched all of it with a patience Hale had taken, his whole life, for weakness. He felt the nights Wendell lay awake over the accounts. He felt the bad investment, the shame of it, the months of covering losses with his own money so Hale wouldn't have to know. He felt the lawyer's letter drafted and torn up six times. And he felt this last afternoon: the envelope in Wendell's breast pocket, a deed, everything signed over, and the small rehearsed speech that went with it.\n\nThen the gun, and his own face behind it, and surprise, and no anger at all.\n\nWendell hit the floor. Perhaps two seconds had passed.\n\nHale stood in the office holding sixty-one years that weren't his. He knew where the envelope was. He couldn't make himself reach for it."}
{"id": "cur-stories-416", "group": "gb-wp-416.txt", "split": "test", "modality": "text", "truth": "ai", "subgroup": "stories/current-model-2026", "source": "written on 2026-11-10 by helper agents of the Claude Code session that built this project (configured model claude-fable-5-2), answering the prompt as they normally would; not told what the detector measures; texts over the length limit were trimmed by the writer", "prompt": "Someone discovers that mirrors lead to a parallel dimension.", "text": "Nadia found out because of the toothpaste. She was brushing her teeth at 5 a.m., half asleep, and a fleck of it landed on the mirror, and her reflection flinched.\n\nShe hadn't.\n\nShe stood very still with the toothbrush in her mouth. The reflection stood still too, but a fraction late, and it had the look of someone who has just dropped a tray in a quiet restaurant.\n\nNadia raised her left hand. The reflection raised its right, correctly. Then she faked a sneeze, stopped halfway, and watched the woman in the glass commit to the whole thing.\n\n\"Ha,\" Nadia said.\n\nThe reflection sagged. Then it put down its toothbrush, leaned forward, and rapped on the glass from the other side.\n\n\"Fine,\" it said. Its voice was hers, as she'd only ever heard it on recordings. \"Fine. You got me. Can I sit down?\"\n\n\"You talk?\"\n\n\"I do all sorts of things. Mostly I do you.\" The reflection lowered itself onto the edge of a bathtub that was the mirror image of Nadia's own. \"Do you have any idea what the hours are like? You get up at five. You check yourself in every shop window on Garrison Street. I have to run to get there first.\"\n\nNadia touched the mirror. It gave under her fingertips, cool and slightly thick, like the skin on paint.\n\n\"There's a whole world back there?\"\n\n\"There's a whole world. We have jobs. This is my job.\" The reflection rubbed its eyes. \"My sister got a woman who doesn't own a full-length mirror. She does forty minutes a day and paints.\"\n\n\"What happens if I come through?\"\n\n\"Honestly? Nobody's ever asked. You all just stand there looking at your own chins.\" It considered her. \"There'd be paperwork. And somebody would have to stay on this side and be you, for the look of the thing.\"\n\nNadia thought about her morning. The 6:10 train. The quarterly review.\n\n\"You'd have to go to a meeting,\" she said.\n\nThe reflection looked at the glass between them, and at the gray window behind Nadia, where it was starting to rain.\n\n\"Is there coffee?\" it asked."}
